ructure, among others, subject to satisfying certain criteria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We are required to comply with the PSL requirements as of March 31 of each fiscal year, a date specified by the RBI for reporting. The assessment of whether we have achieved the PSL requirements is made at the end of the fiscal year based on the average of priority sector target/sub-target achievement as at the end of each quarter. On the basis of the quarterly average, the Bank was slightly short of its total priority sector lending ('PSL') requirement in fiscal 2021. The Bank's total PSL achievement for fiscal 2021 stood at 38.2 percent. Lending to micro enterprises stood at 6.8 percent as against the requirement of 7.5 percent. Advances to the agriculture sector were 10.3 percent of ANBC compared to the requirement of 18.0 percent and agricultural advances made to small and marginal farmers were 2.1 percent of ANBC, compared to the requirement of 8.0 percent. The shortfall in achievement of total PSL and micro enterprises was primarily the result of announcements in fiscal 2021 by the Government stating that MSMEs comprised of manufacturers and service providers and did not include retail and wholesale traders. This adversely impacted the bank's achievement of its PSL target because prior to the change our lending to wholesale and retail traders was considered as part of PSL achievement. Representations were made by various industry bodies in this regard for reinstatement of this segment, considering that traders are an important link in the overall supply chain of the economy. In July 2021, the Government of India decided to reinstate retail and wholesale trade as MSME for priority sector lending and effectively restored the status quo. The Bank is in the process of implementing the decision in this regard and seeking clarification on its applicability for fiscal 2021. See '\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supervision and Regulation-Directed Lending\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 '.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PSL master circular mentions that Scheduled Commercial Banks having any shortfall in lending to priority sector shall be allocated amounts for contribution to the RIDF established with NABARD and other Funds with NABARD, NHB, SIDBI or MUDRA Ltd., as decided by the RBI from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We may be required by the RBI to deposit with the Indian Development Banks certain amounts as specified by the RBI in the coming year due to the shortfall in the above-mentioned sub-categories of priority sector lending targets. As of March 31, 2021, our total investments as directed by RBI in such deposits were Rs. 93.2 billion yielding returns ranging from 2.3 percent to 7.0 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth, for the periods indicated, our loans, broken down by sector, forming part of our directed lending:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                      As of March 31,  \par                                      2017                            2018                     2019         2020              2021   \par                                      (in millions)    \par Directed lending:                                                     \par Agriculture                           Rs.             631,861.6            Rs.   735,135.0            Rs.   742,724.9         Rs.   823,267.2         Rs.   929,043.1     \par Micro small and medium enterprises                    785,715.9                  814,006.9                  1,168,199.9             1,438,145.6             1,359,235.7   \par Other                                                 223,502.7                  205,145.0                  295,582.8               321,819.3               326,168.7     \par Total directed lending                Rs.             1,641,080.2          Rs.   1,754,286.9          Rs.   2,206,507.6       Rs.   2,583,232.1       Rs.   2,614,447.5   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-Performi