 losses on our off-balance sheet credit exposures and undrawn commitments. Accrued expenses and other liabilities include our allowance for credit losses on our off-balance sheet credit exposures and undrawn commitments, aggregating Rs. 5.6 billion as of March 31, 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shareholders' equity increased on the exercise of 29,490,022 stock options by employees and on an increase in our retained earnings, partially offset by a decrease in the balance of our accumulated other comprehensive income. One ADS continues to represent three equity shares.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We are a banking company within the meaning of the Indian Banking Regulation Act, 1949, registered with and subject to supervision by the RBI. Failure to meet minimum capital requirements could lead to regulatory actions by the RBI that, if undertaken, could have a material effect on our financial position. The RBI issued detailed guidelines for implementation of Basel III capital regulations in May 2012. The minimum capital requirements under Basel III are being phased-in as per the guidelines prescribed by the RBI. Accordingly, we are required to maintain a minimum Common Equity Tier I ratio of 7.575 percent, a minimum total Tier I capital ratio of 9.075 percent and a minimum total capital ratio of 11.075 percent (each including capital conservation buffer and additional capital applicable to us as a D-SIB) as of March 31, 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our regulatory capital and capital adequacy ratios measured in accordance with Indian GAAP and calculated under Basel III as of March 31, 2020 and March 31, 2021 are as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                 As of March 31,                   \par                                                 2020                                               2021                      2021  \par                                                 (in million, except percentages)  \par Tier I capital                                   Rs.                              1,714,144.4            Rs.   1,985,873.6           US$   27,151.7    \par Tier II capital                                                                   128,434.1                    139,589.4                   1,908.5     \par Total capital                                   Rs.                              1,842,578.5            Rs.   2,125,463.0           US$   29,060.2     \par Total risk weighted assets                       Rs.                              9,947,157.4            Rs.   11,311,438.8          US$   154,654.6   \par Capital ratios of the Bank:                                                       \par Common Equity Tier I                                                              16.43         %              16.85          %            16.85       % \par Tier I                                                                            17.23         %              17.56          %            17.56       % \par Total capital                                                                     18.52         %              18.79          %            18.79       % \par Minimum capital ratios required by the RBI:*                                      \par Tier I                                                                            9.075         %              9.075          %            9.075       % \par Total capital                                                                     11.075        %              11.075         %            11.075      % \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * The Tier I and Total capital ratio includes capital conservation buffer and additional capital applicable to us as a D-SIB.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our capital expenditures consist principally of expenditures relating to our branch network expansion, as well as investments in our technology and communications infrastructure. Our capital expenditure was Rs. 17.7 billion in fiscal 2021. We have current plans for capital expenditures of approximately Rs. 22.8 billion in fiscal 2022. Our budgeted capital expenditure is primarily to expand our branch and ATM network, to upgrade and expand our hardware, data center, network and other systems, to add new equipment in and expand our existing premises and to relocate our branches and back-offices. We may use these budgeted amounts for other purposes depending on, among other factors the business environment prevailing at the time, and consequently our actual capital expenditures may be higher or lower than our budgeted amounts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 See also '\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk Factors-A slowdown in economic growth in India would cause us to experience slower growth in our asset portfolio and deterioration in the quality of our assets', '-Financial instability in other countries may cause increased volatility in the Indian financial markets' and '-The COVID-19 pandemic may have a material adverse effect on our business, financial condition and results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 '.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial Instruments and Off-Balance Sheet Arrangements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and derivative product offerings to our customers cover a range of products, including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and interest rate transactions and hedging solutions, such as spot and forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts, forward rate agreements, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options, and interest rate derivatives. These transactions enable our customers to transfer, modify or reduce their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and interest rate risks. A specified group of relationship managers from our Treasury front office works on such product offerings jointly with the relationship managers from Wholesale Banking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We enter into forward exchange contracts, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options, forward rate agreements, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps and rupee interest rate swaps with inter-bank participants, similar to our Wholesale Banking business, where we enter into such transactions with our customers. To support our clients' activities, we are an active participant in the Indian inter-bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. We also trade, to a more limited extent, for our own account. We also engage in proprietary trades of rupee-based interest rate swaps and use them as part of our asset liability management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forward exchange contracts are commitments to buy or sell foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a future date at the contracted rate. A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  option is a contract where the purchaser of the option has the right but not the obligation to either purchase or sell and the seller of the option agrees to sell or purchase an agreed amount of a specified \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a price agreed in advance and denominated in another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a specified date or by an agreed date in the future. A forward rate agreement is a financial contract between two parties to exchange interest payments for a 'notional principal' amount on a settlement date, for a specified period from a start date to a maturity date. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps are commitments to exchange cash flows by way of interest in one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and exchanges of principal amounts at maturity (or on specified intermittent dates) based on predetermined rates. Rupee interest rate swaps are commitments to exchange fixed and floating rate cash flows in rupees.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We earn profit on customer transactions by way of a margin as a mark-up over the inter-bank exchange or interest rate. We earn profit on inter-bank transactions by way of a spread between the purchase rate and the sale rate. These profits are recorded as income from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and derivative transactions. Our Board of Directors imposes limits on our ability to hold overnight positions in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and derivatives and the same are intimated to RBI. The following table presents the aggregate notional principal amounts of the our outstanding forward exchange and derivative contracts as of March 31, 2021, together with the fair values on each reporting date:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                              As of March 31, 2021  \par                              Notional                             Gross Assets                  Gross Liabilities         Net Fair Value     Notional                Net Fair Value  \par                              (In million)          \par Interest rate derivatives     Rs.                  3,155,410.2                  Rs.   32,217.2                       Rs.   33,894.0           Rs.      (1,676.8   )                  US$   43,142.1       US$   (22.9   ) \par Forward rate agreements                            20,887.8                           270.0                                64.1                        205.9                               285.6       