                                            \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Quality Indicators\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank monitors the credit quality of its investment securities through various risk management procedures, including the monitoring of credit ratings. A majority of the debt securities in the Bank's investment portfolio were issued by Government of India or State government or entities or agencies that are either explicitly or implicitly guaranteed by such governments. The Bank believes the long history of no credit losses on these securities indicates that the expectation of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 non-payment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the amortized cost basis is zero, even if such governments were to technically default. Therefore, for the aforementioned securities, the Bank does not assess, or record expected credit losses due to the zero loss assumption. The Bank monitors the credit quality of its remaining AFS investment portfolio which are updated periodically. Such of the remaining AFS investment portfolio in an unrealized loss position are evaluated to determine if the loss is attributable to credit related factors and if an allowance for credit loss is needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The average credit rating of the securities comprising the Mortgaged-backed securities and Asset-backed securities was AAA (based upon external ratings where available) as at March 31, 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The contractual residual maturity of available for sale debt securities other than asset and mortgage-backed securities as of March 31, 2021 is set out below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                     As of March 31, 2021  \par                                     Amortized Cost                       Fair Value                   Fair Value  \par                                     (In millions)         \par Within one year                     Rs.                   847,885.7                 Rs.  849,275.7                  US$   11,6