K defined benefit schemes are administered by a separate fund that is legally separated from the Company. The trustees of the pension scheme, are required by law to act in the interest of the fund, and of all relevant stakeholders in the scheme, and are responsible for the investment policy with regard to the assets of the schemes and all other governance matters. The board of trustees must be composed of representatives of the Company and plan participants in accordance with the plan's regulations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Through its defined benefit pension plans, the Company is exposed to a number of risks, the most significant of which are detailed below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asset volatility\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The plan liabilities are calculated using a discount rate set with references to corporate bond yields; if plan assets under perform compared to the corporate bonds discount rate, this will create or increase a deficit. The defined benefit plans hold a significant proportion of equity type assets, which are expected to outperform corporate bonds in the long-term while providing volatility and risk in the short-term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the plans mature, the Company intends to reduce the level of investment risk by investing more in assets that better match the liabilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the Company believes that due to the long-term nature of the plan liabilities and the strength of the supporting group, a level of continuing equity type investments is an appropriate element of the Company's long term strategy to manage the plans efficiently.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Changes in bond yields\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A decrease in corporate bond yields will increase plan liabilities, although this is expected to be partially offset by an increase in the value of the plans' bond holdings and interest rate hedging instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some of the Company's pension obligations are linked to inflation, and higher inflation will lead to higher liabilities (although, in most cases, caps on the level of inflationary increases are in place to protect the plan against high inflation). The plans hold a significant proportion of assets in index linked gilts, together with other inflation hedging instruments and also assets which are more closely correlated with inflation. However an increase in inflation will also increase the deficit to some degree.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Life expectancy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The majority of the plan's obligations are to provide benefits for the life of the member, so increases in life expectancy will result in an increase in the plan's liabilities. This is particularly significant in the UK defined benefit plans, where inflationary increases result in higher sensitivity to changes in life expectancy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-82\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets out the disclosure pertaining to employee benefits of Jaguar Land Rover Limited\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                                                  \par                                                                                                  As at March 31,    \par                                                                                                  Pension benefits   \par                                                                                                  2021                              2021                     2020   \par                                                                                                  (In millions)      \par Change in defined benefit obligation:                                                                               \par Defined benefit obligation, beginning of the year                                                US$                9,963.4               Rs.   728,421.5            