                                            \par Gain/(loss) reclassified fromother comprehensive income to the consolidated income statement           US$                    (139.6   )          Rs.   (10,201.3)            Rs.   (49,160.5   )     Rs.   (80,562.9   ) \par                                                                                                                                                                                                             \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-98\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial risk management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the course of its business, the Company is exposed primarily to fluctuations in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates, interest rates, equity prices, liquidity and credit risk, which may adversely impact the fair value of its financial instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company has a risk management policy which not only covers the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks but also other risks associated with the financial assets and liabilities such as interest rate risks and credit risks. The risk management policy is approved by the board of directors. The risk management framework aims to:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Create a stable business planning environment by reducing the impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and interest rate fluctuations on the Company's business plan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Achieve greater predictability to earnings by determining the financial value of the expected earnings in advance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i) Market risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market risk is the risk of any loss in future earnings, in realizable fair values or in future cash flows that may result from a change in the price of a financial instrument. The value of a financial instrument may change as a result of changes in the interest rates, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates, equity price fluctuations, liquidity and other market changes. Future specific market movements cannot be normally predicted with reasonable accuracy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i)-(a) Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate risk:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fluctuation in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates may have potential impact on the consolidated income statement, consolidated statement of comprehensive income ,consolidated balance sheet, consolidated statement of changes in equity and cosolidated statement of cash flows, where any transaction references more than one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or where assets/liabilities are denominated in a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the respective consolidated entities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Considering the countries and economic environment in which the Company operates, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are subject to risks arising from fluctuations in exchange rates in those countries. The risks primarily relate to fluctuations in U.S. dollar, GBP, Chinese renminbi, Japanese yen, Singapore dollar and Euro, against the respective functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Tata Motors Limited and its subsidiaries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company, as per its risk management policy, uses \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other derivative instruments primarily to hedge \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and interest rate exposure. Furthermore, any movement in the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Company against major foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may impact the Company's revenues and expenditure relating to its international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Any weakening of the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may impact the Company's cost of imports and cost of borrowings and consequently may increase the cost of financing the Company's capital expenditures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company evaluates the impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate fluctuations by assessing its exposure to exchange rate risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate sensitivity is calculated for each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by aggregation of the net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate exposure of each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a simultaneous parallel \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates shift in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates of each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by 10% while keeping the other variables as constant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-99\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following analysis is based on the gross exposure as of the relevant balance sheet dates, which could affect the income statement. There is no exposure to the income statement on account of translation of financial statements of consolidated foreign entities. Furthermore, the exposure as indicated below is mitigated by some of the derivative contracts entered into by the Company as disclosed at clause (iv) below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth information relating to foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure (other than risk arising from derivatives disclosed at clause (iv) below) as at March 31, 2021:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                                  \par                          U.S. dollar                      Euro                    ChineseRenminbi       GBP                Canadiandollar                   Others*                   Total   \par                          (In millions)   \par Financial assets                         Rs.181,174.9              Rs.113,988.0                         Rs. 34,468.8                        Rs. 7,817.4                 Rs. 4,761.5               Rs.26,522.2         Rs. 368,732.8    \par Financial liabilities                    Rs.480,423.3              Rs.433,444.1                         Rs.120,331.7                        Rs.56,714.0                 Rs.13,253.5               Rs.28,956.2         Rs.1,133,122.8   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Others mainly include \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Russian rouble, Singapore dollars, Swiss franc, Australian dollars, South African rand, Thai baht, Japanese Yen and Korean won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below outlines the effect change in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure for the year ended March 31, 2021:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                            \par Change in assumption                         Impact on Company'snet income before taxfor financial assets     Impact onCompany's netincome before taxfor financialliabilities \par Appreciation in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b