c2 \f2 F-100\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below outlines the effect change in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure for the year ended March 31, 2019:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                              \par Change in assumption                         Impact on Company's netincome before taxfor financial assets     Impact onCompany's netincome before taxfor financialliabilities     Impact onCompany's othercomprehensiveincome for financialliabilities \par Appreciation in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by 10%    Increase byRs. 42,004.4 million                                  Decrease byRs. (77,446.6) million                                   Decrease byRs. (6,914.9) million \par Depreciation in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by 10%    Decrease byRs. (42,004.4) million                                Increase byRs. 77,446.6 million                                     Increase byRs. 6,914.9 million \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Note: The impact is indicated on the income/loss before tax basis).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-101\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i) - (b) Interest rate risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Interest rate risk is the risk that changes in market interest rates will lead to changes in fair value of financial instruments or changes in interest income, expense and cash flows of the Company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company is subject to variable interest rates on some of its interest bearing liabilities. The Company's interest rate exposure is mainly related to debt obligations. The Company also uses a mix of interest rate sensitive financial instruments to manage the liquidity and fund requirements for its day to day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like short term non-convertible bonds and short term loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its financing business, the Company enters into transactions with customers which primarily result in receivables at fixed rates. In order to manage this risk, the Company has a policy to match funding in terms of maturities and interest rates and also for certain part of the portfolio, the Company does not match funding with maturities, in order to take advantage of market opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company also enters into arrangements of securitization of receivables in order to reduce the impact of interest rate movements. Further, the Company also enters in to interest rate swap contracts with banks to manage its interest rate risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at March 31, 2021, 2020 and 2019, financial liabilities of Rs. 465,893.8 million, Rs. 450,211.5 million and Rs. 302,786.3 million, respectively, were subject to variable interest rates. Increase/decrease of 100 basis points in interest rates at the balance sheet date would result in an impact (decrease/increase of net income ) of Rs. 4,687.8 million, Rs. 4,502.1 million and Rs. 3,027.9 million on income for the years ended March 31, 2021, 2020 and 2019, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The model assumes that interest rate changes are instantaneous parallel shifts in the yield curve. Although some assets and liabilities may have similar maturities or periods to re-pricing, these may not react correspondingly to changes in market interest rates. Also, the interest rates on some types of assets and liabilities may fluctuate with changes in market interest rates, while interest rates on other types of assets may change with a lag.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk estimates provided assume a parallel shift of 100 basis points interest rate across all yield curves. This calculation also assumes that the change occurs at the balance sheet date and has been calculated based on risk exposures outstanding as at that date. The period end balances are not necessarily representative of the average debt outstanding during the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This analysis assumes that all other variables, in particular foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, remain constant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company uses cross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rate swaps to hedge some of its exposure to interest rate arising from variable rate foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated debt. The Company also uses cross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rate swaps to convert some of its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated fixed rate debt to floating rate debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (i) - (c) Equity Price risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Equity Price Risk is related to the change in market reference price of the investments in equity securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fair value of some of the Company's investments in equity securities exposes the Company to equity price risks. In general, these securities are not held for trading purposes. These investments are subject to changes in the market price of securities. The fair value of quoted equity securities measured at FVOCI as at March 31, 2021, 2020 and 2019, was Rs.4,993.7 million, Rs.1,586.8 million and Rs.3,043.1 million, respectively. A 10% change in equity prices of quoted equity securities measured at FVOCI held as at March 31, 2021, 2020 and 2019, would result in a pre-tax impact of Rs.499.4 million, Rs.158.7 million and Rs.304.3 million on equity, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fair value of the Company's investments in quoted equity securities measured at FVTPL as of March 31, 2021 and 2020, was Rs. Nil and Rs.1,577.8 million , respectively. A 10% change in prices of these securities measured at FVTPL held as of March 31, 2021 and 2020, would result in an impact of Rs.Nil and Rs.157.8 million on profit before tax respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 F-102\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ii)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk is the risk of financial loss arising from counterparty failure to repay or service debt according to the contractual terms or obligations. Credit risk encompasses both the direct risk of default and the risk of deterioration of creditworthiness as well as concentration risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial instruments that are subject to concentrations of credit risk, principally consist of investments in debt instruments, trade receivables, finance receivables, loans and advances and derivative financial instruments. The Company strives to promptly identify and reduce concerns about collection due to a deterioration in the financial conditions and others of its main counterparties by regularly monitoring their situation based on their financial condition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 None of the financial instruments of the Company result in material concentrations of credit risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The carrying amount of financial assets represents the maximum credit exposure. The maximum exposure to credit risk was Rs. 1,278,725.2 million as at March 31, 2021 and Rs. 9,83,045.4 million as at March 31, 2020, being the total of the carrying amount of balances with banks, short term deposits with banks, trade receivables, finance receivables, margin money and other financial assets excluding equity investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial assets that are neither past due nor impaired\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 None of the Company's cash equivalents, including time deposits with banks, are past due or impaired. Regarding trade receivables and other receivables, and other loans or receivables that are neither impaired nor past due, there were no indications as at March 31, 2021, and March 31,2020, that defaults in payment obligations will occur.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit quality of financial assets and impairment loss\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ageing of trade receivables and finance receivables as of balance sheet date is given below. The age analysis have been considered from the due date\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                               As at March 31,   \par                               2021                          2021                       2021           2021          2021                      2021                     2020            2020              2020   \par Trade receivables             Gross                         Allowance                  Net            Gross         Allowance                 Net                      Gross           Allowance         Net    \par                               (In millions)     \par Period (in months)                                                                            \par Not due                       US$               1,408.4                 US$   (4.2     )        US$   1,404.2       Rs.         102,967.2            Rs.   (307.8)               Rs.   102,659.4         Rs.    81,991.8        Rs.   (330.3)            Rs.   81,661.5    \par Overdue up to 3 months                          197.8                         (4.0     )              193.8                     14,457.8                   (291.1      )               14,166.7                 19,802.0              (163.8       )           19,638.2    \par Overdue 3-6 months                              33.6                          (0.9     )              32.7                      2,466.9                    (69.3       )               2,397.6                  3,635.8               (372.1       )           3,263.7     \par Overdue more than 6 months                      229.7                         (126.2   )              103.5                     16,790.8                   (9,223.7    )               7,567.1     *            17,437.3              (10,273.8    )           7