before basing \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon it is trading around 1.2125 \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at 10-day MA at 1.2085 and break would ease upward pressure \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 76.4 of the 1.2349/1.1704 move at 1.2196 \u8226\'20\'22 Market is long EUR/USD and unless 1.2200 breaks soon a correction may ensue AUD/USD-Breaks lower as Asian equity markets slump \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.14% higher despite soft equities and higher US yields \u8226\'20\'22 From the opening level at 0.7842 - the AUD/USD slipped lower in early Asia \u8226\'20\'22 Losses accelerated late morning when the Taiwan stock market plunged over 8% \u8226\'20\'22 Fall was due to report Taiwan may raise COVID alert as infections rise \u8226\'20\'22 AXJ equity index fell over 1.3% and risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  went offered \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD fell below support at 0.7815/25 to a low so far at 0.7795 \u8226\'20\'22 It is trading just above the low heading into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at the 10-day MA at 0.7780 and 21-day MA at 0.7764 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 76.4 of the 0.8007/0.7532 move at 0.7895 GBP/USD-Soft on safe haven USD demand into the UK data dump \u8226\'20\'22 -0.15% - capped by lower stocks, as Asia buys the safe haven USD \u8226\'20\'22 UK unveils law to fine social media firms which fail to remove online abuse \u8226\'20\'22 Plan joins those in other nations, wrestling with social media \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, momentum studies 5, 10 & 21 DMAs plus 21 day Bolli bands head higher \u8226\'20\'22 Setup suggests further topside gains - 1.4240 2021 high is the next stop \u8226\'20\'22 Asian 1.4146 high is initial resistance, London 1.4105 base supports \u8226\'20\'22 Cable eases from overbought - rising upper 21 day Bollinger band at 1.4121 \u8226\'20\'22 Extensive UK data, led by GDP and IP, is event risk in London Gold price may show the way for the dollar Spot gold is trading just below key technical levels and the price action in the next few days may point the direction for the U.S. dollar, as the inverse correlation between the two has strengthened since inflation concerns began gripping investors in all asset classes. The USD has suffered due to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's insistence that near-term inflation pressures are transitory and it does not need to act pre-emptively, while gold has benefited due to its traditional role as a hedge against inflation. Spot gold is trending higher with the 5, 10 and 21-day moving averages aligned in a bullish formation. It faces resistance at $1,845-1,850 where the 200 DMA and the 61.8 Fibonacci retracement of the 2021 high/low converge. More resistance comes in at $1,875, a double-top from late January. A break above $1,880 should cause the uptrend to accelerate and bring the year's $1,959 high into focus. There is good support for gold at $1,788 where the 21 DMA converges with a trend-line drawn from the March low at $1,676. A break below $1,775 would suggest a short-term top is in place and a correction lower will follow. The dollar index is trending lower with the 5, 10 and 21 DMAs aligned in a bearish formation. The target of the current trend is the 2021 low at 89.20. Whether it falls there swiftly without correcting first, may depend on the price action in spot gold. INR recovery impressive but unlikely to last The Indian rupee's impressive 2.8% recovery from its April low of 75.41 looks increasingly fragile as the coronavirus crisis threatens India's economic recovery, while rising U.S. yields, heightened risk aversion and the likelihood of \uc2 Reserve bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  make for a negative backdrop. With \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seven-day average of new cases at a record high, calls have increased for a national lockdown and a growing number of states have imposed tougher restrictions, prompting GDP and earnings downgrades by analysts. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  will revisit its April forecast for 12.5% growth in India's economic output in fiscal years 2021 and 2022 in July. Rising U.S inflation expectations due to surging prices of commodities, houses and other goods and services, and growing price pressures in China have driven bond yields higher globally. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield has stabilised above 1.60%, prompting a selloff on Wall Street - both negative factors for the INR. Given the RBI's emphasis on rupee stability, it is unlikely to allow a rapid INR rise; there were reports of dollar bids from state-run banks at 73.35 on Monday, sparking talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/INR is likely to hold strong support at 73.00-06 and trading a 73.00-75.00 range may prove prudent.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-5-15T04:56:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210512eh5c00n2l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
