\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 4-India's retail inflation rises to 5.03% in February\b0\par\par\uc2 1,081 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 March 12 (Reuters) - India's retail inflation accelerated to a three-month high in February on higher fuel prices, which could challenge the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's accommodative stance and record-low key policy rates adopted to boost the pandemic-hit economy.\par \par \uc2 Annual retail inflation rose 5.03% in February, up from 4.06% in January, data released by the Ministry of Statistics showed on Friday. Analysts in a Reuters poll had predicted annual inflation of 4.83% for February.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY\par \par \uc2 RADHIKA RAO\uc2 , \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2 , \uc2 DBS BANK\uc2 , SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "Improving growth expectations and firm crude prices are complicating monetary policy expectations. The one-year implied rate has risen this year, as have 5-year overnight index swaps, as markets build policy tightening risks.\par \par \uc2 Bounce in February's inflation ... adds to this debate. Much of the increase stemmed from a sequential uptick in staple vegetables and commodity price pressures. With part of the pass-through also seeping into services (transport), core inflation hardened to 5.9% y/y, spurred also by a return in the demand impulse and return in producers' pricing power.\par \par \uc2 To douse premature rate tightening expectations, the RBI is likely to reassert its accommodative stance on rates as well as place liquidity normalisation on a slow gear. Firmer USD induced \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness has also partially eased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures."\par \par \uc2 KUNAL KUNDU, \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2 , \uc2 SOCIETE GENERALE\uc2 , BENGALURU\par \par \uc2 "While the low statistical base effect is partly responsible for this uptick, there are telltale signs of price pressures building up, notably through petro (petroleum) product prices bus and airfare segment.\par \par \uc2 Price pressure is also building up from manufactured product price (driven by a sharp rise in commodity prices) and house price channel. Continued stickiness of core inflation adds to the concern.\par \par \uc2 Also, despite weak business, recreation and amusement prices continue to move up as service providers' needs factor in rising costs to prepare for the pandemic-engendered new normal. If the underlying price pressure continues to remain elevated even when the base effect normalises, there is a high possibility of RBI opting for a rate hike sooner than we expect."\par \par \uc2 SAKSHI GUPTA, SENIOR \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2 , \uc2 HDFC BANK\uc2 , GURUGRAM\par \par \uc2 "Inflation inched higher in the month of February to 5% from 4.06% in January on the back of rising fuel prices and inflationary pressures in certain food items like onions, meat and eggs. As we had suspected, perhaps the low point for inflation is behind us for some months to come.\par \par \uc2 From a monetary policy perspective, the rising inflationary risks although still nascent could trigger some caution from the RBI.\par \par \uc2 That said, we continue to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping monetary policy accommodative and focus towards managing yields and supporting growth for now. We expect CPI to average at 5-5.5% in H1 FY22."\par \par \uc2 MADHAVI ARORA\uc2 , LEAD \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2 , \uc2 EMKAY GLOBAL FINANCIAL SERVICES LTD\uc2 , MUMBAI\par \par \uc2 "The February CPI inflation uptick after having seen downward surprises would imply that the revised Q4 FY21 estimate of RBI at 5.2% could see further downside of 30 bps or so.\par \par \uc2 If food inflation normalizes in the next year to sub-3% (from 8%+ in FY21E), the headline inflation could average comfortably below 4.5% in FY22E, compared with ~6.2% in FY21E. However, risks of increasing input costs, higher commodity prices and seasonal upside in food prices and better pricing power remain key risks to inflation.\par \par \uc2 We see core inflation outdoing headline inflation through most parts of FY22. While this could worry the policymakers, the policy stance will likely remain accommodative both on rates and liquidity front in CY21."\par \par \uc2 GARIMA KAPOOR, \uc2 ECONOMIST\uc2  - INSTITUTIONAL EQUITIES, ELARA CAPITAL, MUMBAI\par \par \uc2 "Today's CPI print clearly reflects the impact of higher fuel prices and lower sequential decline in food prices amid waning effect of seasonality. While the headline CPI numbers post March-2021 will begin to moderate amid base effect, core inflation is expected to remain firm amid higher commodity and especially crude prices.\par \par \uc2 We expect global crude oil prices to remain elevated at least for another 4-5 months owing to the widening gap between global oil demand and supply. Given that the government is likely to overachieve its FY21 revenue target, a likely cut in excise duty may be on the cards."\par \par \uc2 SUJAN HAJRA\uc2 , CHIEF ECONOMIST, ANAND RATHI SECURITIES, MUMBAI "The numbers were slightly higher than expected. We see CPI around 4%-5% this year and broadly speaking the numbers are in that range.\par \par \uc2 While food inflation is our major concern going forward, we see a selective increase in the non-food category, especially transport and communication due to higher fuel prices and their impact on other manufactured products.\par \par \uc2 However, the 5% range is pretty much in the comfort zone of RBI, and expect them to maintain the rate pause as well as accommodative liquidity stance."\par \par \uc2 SUMAN CHOWDHURY, CHIEF ANALYTICAL OFFICER, ACUIT\u201\'00\'C9 RATINGS & RESEARCH, MUMBAI\par \par \uc2 "The sharp reversal in the CPI inflation trajectory to 5.03% in Feb 2021 from 4.06% in the previous month reflects the potential inflationary pressures in the economy.\par \par \uc2 The sharp drop in food inflation to 2.67% in January 2021, which was largely on account of a favourable base (high vegetable prices in January 2020), has been partly reversed to 4.25% due to continuing high inflation in edible oil, animal protein and pulses.\par \par \uc2 The CPI print reinforces concerns on a further increase in core inflation amidst the ongoing economic revival. RBI is expected to keep a close eye on the inflation trajectory despite its strong focus on economic revival, and suitably calibrate its monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 ADITI NAYAR\uc2 , PRINCIPAL ECONOMIST, ICRA, GURUGRAM\par \par \uc2 "We expect the CPI inflation to rise further in March 2021, before recording a base-effect led dip in April 2021, reflecting the impact of the spike in inflation during the lockdown.\par \par \uc2 We continue to expect a status quo on the repo rate through 2021, with a rapidly dimming likelihood of an early change in stance from accommodative to neutral."\par \par \uc2 RUPA REGE NITSURE\uc2 , GROUP CHIEF ECONOMIST, L&T FINANCIAL HOLDINGS, MUMBAI\par \par \uc2 "At this juncture, the decision of the monetary policy committee will be driven more by weak growth, high unemployment & large sized govt borrowings programme than by cost push inflationary pressures." (Reporting by Anuron Kumar Mitra, Sachin Ravikumar, Nallur Sethuraman, Rama Venkat, Chris Thomas, Philip George and Nivedita Bhattacharjee in Bengaluru and Swati Bhat in Mumbai; Editing by Vinay Dwivedi, Devika Syamnath & Aditya Soni) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-12T14:41:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210312eh3c01ki2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
