\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  windfalls trigger surprise rate cuts -- expect more\b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2020\par 11:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nov 19 (Reuters) - Today's surprise rates cuts are probably the product of unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation that could lead to further easing, supporting risk appetite and gains for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher yields.\par \par \uc2 This benevolent circle could last for some time, but there's clear risk that demand for currently higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will wane as rates fall. The seeds of a reverse are already being sown.\par \par \uc2 Philippine and Indonesian central banks unexpectedly cut rates today. PHP has risen 7.7% from its March low; IDR has gained 14.7%, suppressing inflation and providing room to ease. With similar gains for other perceived risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more surprise rate cuts may follow.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets today, with rates forecast unchanged, but ZAR has gained 5% since the last rate decision, in September.\par \par \uc2 China may matter most and could utilize CNH's 5% rise in the past 16 weeks to ease.\par \par \uc2 Central Banks with no room to cut, or in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case, fighting inflation have turned to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  For more click on FXBUZ\par \par \uc2 (Jeremy Boulton is a Reuters market analyst. The views expressed are his own)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-11-22T10:16:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201119egbj01qnl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichg