\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Indian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2 's loosening of rupee reins likely to aid more gains\b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2020\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Tuesday story without changes to text)\par \par \uc2 * High retail inflation prompting RBI to hold off \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * RBI likely to opt for supporting bonds over rupee in near-term\par \par \uc2 * Rupee may trade in 72-74/dollar range over 3-6 months\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 1 (Reuters) - \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have stopped trying to rein in the rupee to help exports, letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drift to a six-month high and raising expectations it will gain further, analysts and market participants said.\par \par \uc2 Dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 in recent months has made the rupee one of the worst-performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2020, despite massive dollar inflows into stock markets and for corporate fundraising.\par \par \uc2 The RBI has kept the rupee weak to help exporters and made large interest rate cuts to support the economy as the coronavirus pandemic curbs activity, but inflation risks are now putting it in a bind.\par \par \uc2 The government's decision to sharply increase market borrowing while the economy and public finances are under strain is driving up bond yields, which move inversely to prices.\par \par \uc2 Dollar weakness, foreign direct investment and stock market inflows, meanwhile, have all added to the rupee's strength.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tamp down yields, it doesn't have much scope to restrain the rupee without fuelling inflation at a later point.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's highly tactical. For now, yes, the RBI is happy to allow some appreciation as it's running out of space to do unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said A. Prasanna, chief economist at ICICI Securities Primary Dealership.\par \par \uc2 The RBI typically removes excess rupee supply in the banking system from its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling bonds.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, however, the RBI has been unable to sell bonds on the open market, and has instead bought them to combat rising yields.\par \par \uc2 It has been using a strategy inspired by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's '\b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist' programme: buying long-end and selling short-end debt to manage market yields and supporting the government's record borrowing plans.\par \par \uc2 BONDS OVER RUPEE FOR NOW?\par \par \uc2 The RBI may become increasingly constrained when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the rupee, particularly if there is a need to support bond markets through purchases, analysts at Nomura wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The recent rise in retail inflation, reflecting supply bottlenecks due to the lockdown, adds to the RBI's problems.\par \par \uc2 While announcing market stability measures on Monday, the RBI said the recent appreciation of the rupee should help contain imported inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 "I doubt the appreciation will have an impact on inflation but it's a nice cover for RBI to maintain a hands-free approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ICICI PD's Prasanna said.\par \par \uc2 Traders are happy with the shift in RBI's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  management strategy and said the rupee will catch up with regional peers and benefit from broad dollar weakness and inflows. They are, however, unsure if the RBI is likely to stay out completely.\par \par \uc2 Some believe the RBI may continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forwards market to indirectly influence spot levels, while others say it will continue to want to build \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  said it sees scope for the dollar to stabilise after the notable correction since March, and thus beyond the flows-driven gain in the rupee, a strong and persistent one-sided bias might not be on the cards.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the rupee will continue to appreciate. With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lower end we could see a 72-74 range on the rupee over the next 3-6 months," said Sameer Narang, chief economist at Bank of Baroda. (Reporting by Swati Bhat in Mumbai, Editing by Alasdair Pal and Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-2T00:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200901eg910427y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
