to participate in the analysis for Spain\u180\'00\'B4s economic transformation plan in Madrid \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  and \uc2 Fabio Panetta\uc2  participate via videoconference in Eurogroup meeting in Frankfurt Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY heavy but tethered to options, crosses mixed \u8226\'20\'22 Story o/n was risk-on, broadly weaker USD, weak JPY except against USD \u8226\'20\'22 Consolidation name of game in Asia, USD/JPY 107.18-36, heavy but supports \u8226\'20\'22 Nearby option expiries today supportive, tether, total USD1.3 bln 107.00-35 \u8226\'20\'22 More above - 107.50 USD1.1 bln, 107.55-85 992 mln, tom 107.15-25 972 mln \u8226\'20\'22 Tokyo risk mood neutral, Nikkei @22,486, US yields too, Tsy 10s @0.662% \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 121.59-76, better bid just below Monday's 121.70 high \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/JPY 74.50-95, resistance/offers still from @75.00 \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/JPY 135.13-40, quiet, holding at recent highs, bias? EUR/USD-Poised to move higher on break above 1.1355 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD has finally broken above 1.1355 on fifth try in 17 trading days \u8226\'20\'22 The break higher targets the June 10 trend high at 1.1422 \u8226\'20\'22 Broad USD weakness and buoyant equity markets underpinning EUR/USD \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD buyers tipped between 1.1315/30 on any dips \u8226\'20\'22 Combination of buoyant risk and US growth concerns on coronavirus surge underpin AUD/USD-Soft USD continues to underpin \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.50% higher at 0.6980 after USD broadly weakened \u8226\'20\'22 It moved up to 0.6995 in early Asia before sellers ahead of 0.7000 capped \u8226\'20\'22 The failure to break 0.7000 led to some longs paring back \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded to 0.6969 before steadying and pushing back above 0.6980 \u8226\'20\'22 There wasn't any reaction to the China inflation data \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD was more influenced by the tone in equity markets \u8226\'20\'22 A break above 0.7000 targets the June 10 high at 0.7069 \u8226\'20\'22 Hourly support around 0.6950 with the 10-day MA at 0.6930 GBP/USD-Remains bid, but 1.2700 looks a tough nut to crack \u8226\'20\'22 Touch former in a 1.2605-1.2630 range - busy once Asia fully opened \u8226\'20\'22 Buyers return to COVID-hit UK property market in June - RICS \u8226\'20\'22 Techs; momentum studies climb, 5, 10 & 21 DMAs conflict - neutral setup \u8226\'20\'22 1.2668-98 now key resistance, upper 21 day Bolli, 76.4% June fall & 200 DMA \u8226\'20\'22 Lower 21 day Bolli rejected in June, close above 1.2490 21 DMA was positive \u8226\'20\'22 Targets a test of the falling 1.2693 upper 21 day Bollinger band \u8226\'20\'22 London 1.2509 low first support, then 1.2473 10 DMA the pivotal level Scope for a risk correction as dollar holds range base The dollar has been under pressure in July as a rally on Wall Street, fed by better than forecast U.S. data and an unexpectedly strong Chinese economy, underpinned risk appetite. This weighed on the safe-haven dollar, but a round of profit-taking now appears viable as the investor sentiment pendulum swings, a recurring feature of 2020 markets. The risk-negative - and thus dollar-positive - factors are led by the increasingly uncertain longer-term economic and social impact of the expanding coronavirus in the U.S. and globally. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials fear that the U.S. economy may be stalling, which would surprise Wall Street. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government stimulus has negated the impact of the virus, but a second wave cannot be ruled out in several countries, which would hurt risk appetite. The dollar USD tested key support Wednesday at 96.37-96.41, the four-week range base and the 21-day lower Bollinger band, which is a very good indicator of an oversold market. Five, 10 and 21 daily moving averages and momentum studies all provide conflicting signals. Thus technically, the range base has a good chance of holding, with a bounce towards 97.80, the June 30 and four-week range top, viable. As China tries to douse equity euphoria, yuan may shrivel In a classic case of be careful what you wish for, there are signs China's rhetoric-fuelled stock market rally could soon fizzle out, and so too the yuan's jaunt above the 7.0000 level against the U.S. dollar. China's state media on Thursday sought to tamp down raging enthusiasm for stock investments, hinting at concerns about a growing bubble, just days after it triggered a buying spree by touting the benefits of a bull market for the country's economic recovery. The equity euphoria has spurred a 1.2% appreciation of the offshore yuan versus the dollar since last week, as USD/CNH broke the 7.00 psychological support, attracting bets on an extension of the move. The pair set a new four-month low of 6.9872 Thursday, closing in on a key Fibonacci chart support at 6.9800 that could prompt a profit-taking rebound. The Shanghai Composite has pared its early 0.9% rise but is still up 0.2% at mid-morning. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have not yet heeded the warning from China's state media to check their enthusiasm. Perhaps stronger persuasion may be needed. Thursday's USD/CNY midpoint set by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixed the yuan's value slightly weaker than expected, hinting that it could be reeled back in by authorities soon. RBI deftly guides USD/INR into a lower range The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  appears to have recently orchestrated a slightly lower trading range for the U.S. dollar versus the Indian rupee via suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The performance of China's yuan might explain why. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely perceived to have boxed USD/INR into a broad 74.90-76.30 range in May and June. But since early July the lower parameter of the RBI's tolerance level seems to have shifted to 74.35-50, in line with broader dollar weakness. The RBI's reluctance to allow more meaningful INR appreciation despite buoyant global risk appetite and substantial foreign investor inflows marks a departure from its stated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb extreme volatility. But viewed in relation to the Chinese yuan's stability since April, it makes perfect sense. India is keen to attract companies seeking manufacturing sites outside China after the coronavirus pandemic disrupted global supply chains. Hence, the authorities are likely to favour INR stability - if not weakness - versus the CNY to maintain competitiveness. The RBI's dollar buying also creates liquidity and builds up needed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves given India's current account deficit. The yuan's 0.8% appreciation against the dollar since July 1 will provide the RBI some leeway to allow INR appreciation. But a sustained move beyond 74.35, the March 27 rupee high, is unlikely unless accompanied by significant yuan strength.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-7-12T05:11:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200709eg7900ku1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
