1.1050 where 21 & 55-day MAs converge AUD/USD-Pivots around 0.6810 ahead of FOMC \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded in a 0.6805/15 range during a quiet US session \u8226\'20\'22 Market on the sidelines ahead of FOMC later today \u8226\'20\'22 US-China uncertainty capping rallies and weighing on sentiment \u8226\'20\'22 Buyers at 0.6800 underpins with support at 61.8 of 0.6754/0.6862 at 0.6795 \u8226\'20\'22 The 10, 21, 55 & 100-day MAs bunched between 0.6805/15 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at 61.8 of the larger 0.6929/0.6759 move at 0.6862 GBP/USD-Busy on You Gov surprise, but GBP hangs in \u8226\'20\'22 Trades off 0.15% in the middle of a hectic 1.3107/1.3160 range \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP also saw heavy volumes in a 0.8433/0.8461 range \u8226\'20\'22 You Gov poll showed a 28 seat Conservative victory from 68 \u8226\'20\'22 Cable fell from 1.3180 to touch 1.3107, then traded around 1.3130 \u8226\'20\'22 Resilient GBP performance leaves 5, 10 & 21 daily & weekly MAs moving higher \u8226\'20\'22 Sustained 1.3200 break would open the door to the 1.3380 2019 high in March \u8226\'20\'22 1.3100 double daily low initial support - 1.3185 310M & 1.3200 320M strikes \u8226\'20\'22 Boris remains strong favourite, but perhaps not a shoo in INR's impressive gains may be difficult to sustain The Indian rupee has impressed with relatively strong gains of 1.25% versus the USD in December but the chances of it extending the rally appear slim. Analysts suspect that persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is determined to contain a sharp appreciation of the rupee in contrast to its oft stated policy of only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excessive volatility. This subtle policy shift is likely to have been prompted by a desire to keep the rupee competitive versus other Asian peers and should act as a hurdle to further sizable INR gains. RBI's rate cut pause last week citing inflation concerns shifts the onus on to the government to spend more to revive a weakening economy which may lead to fiscal slippage. These fears have driven selling of Indian government bonds and may start to weigh on the INR soon. USD/INR has been boxed in a range of 70.35 to 72.25 since the end of September with sturdy technical support evident in the 70.30-50 area. Strong corporate rupee inflows may result in the USD testing this level but it will be prudent to play the range rather than bet on further sizable INR gains. You Gov poll fuels UK election jitters - GBP is resilient The final You Gov poll/model before the December 12th election showed the Conservative majority slipping from 68 two weeks ago to 28. A You Gov spokesman said, due to the margin of error, a hung parliament cannot be ruled out. Earlier Tuesday a Focalpoint model showed Johnson with a 24 seat majority, down from 82. Cable fell from 1.3180 to touch 1.3107 on You Gov, then showed resilience, recovering to the current 1.3135. It seems some uncommitted remain voters are moving to Labour, but Johnson still has a decent lead. The Conservatives have maintained a focussed campaign, while remain voters have split allegiances. Labours' nationalization plans have spooked industry and Corbyn struggles to connect with swing voters. It would be a big surprise if Boris does not get a working majority. Failure to do so would likely see sterling slump back towards the 1.2000 lows seen in September. A Conservative majority will probably see a knee jerk topside test, towards the 1.3380 2019 high, but there is debate if strength can be sustained. Johnson has to negotiate a trade deal with the \uc2 EU\uc2 , and his inflammatory oratory during the campaign will make it very difficult for them to trust him. USD likely to firm in wake of upbeat FOMC The Fed decision is handed down later today and the market is pricing in no change from the current 1.50-1.75% target. Attention will be on the statement, economic projections, dot-plot and Powell press conference. The Fed is likely to affirm the economy and current monetary policy are \u8220\'20\'1Cin a good place\u8221\'20\'1D. The Fed will likely signal policy on hold for the foreseeable future by acknowledging the labor market remains strong, while inflation will remain close to their target, but contained. The dot-plots will likely adjust 25 BPs lower to reflect the October cut, but the median projections may show no change to policy through 2020. According to FEDWATCH, markets start to price in the chance of a 25 \uc2 BP\uc2  cut in the middle of 2020 and nearly fully price in a cut in Q4 2020. An upbeat Fed message could prompt markets to price out any policy change in 2020, which would underpin USD. The USD index (DXY) is near the bottom of the 97.10/98.35 range, where it has traded since the mid-October and may move toward the upper end following the Fed. A break above 97.80 could lead to a test of the 100-day MA around 98.15.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-12-14T04:31:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191211efcb00dw1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
