\par\par \b \uc2 Costs of a Weaker Rupee Could Outweigh Benefits -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2018\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0734 GMT - With the rupee seemingly hitting record lows daily, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  expects the declines to weigh on the country's economic growth. India has $218 billion of short-term debt obligations due in December, and refinancing that could be a significant cost for the government. That as it's already dealing with a high oil-import bill and rising bond yields. \uc2 SBI\uc2  also believes the traditional view that weak exchange rates could dramatically boost export growth isn't entirely correct as India's basket has changed significantly from traditional products to more-mechanized engineering goods over the years. That makes such wares more income-elastic than price-elastic. The dollar is now at INR72.05. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT - Italian bond yields continue falling, pointing to improving investor confidence over Italy's budget but \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  rates strategists say more facts are needed for the rally to continue. "The BTP [Italian government bond] rally needs official confirmation of the positive budget news, or it risks running into some profit-taking," they say. On Monday, Italian bond prices rose, driving yields lower as markets relieved after \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  had affirmed Italy's 'BBB' rating, albeit with a revision of the outlook to "negative." News later that Italy's government was mulling a budget with a deficit of just above 2% for 2019 gave further boost to Italian bond prices, pushing the yields below 3%. The 10-year BTP yield trades at 2.91%, down 4 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT - Bidding for land parcels auctioned by \uc2 Singapore government\uc2  --the first set since fresh measures to cool the residential market 2 months ago--was "noticeably subdued" and lacked big developers, says DBS. City Developments was the only large local builder to place an unsuccessful bid for any of the 3 parcels, the investment bank notes, adding that foreign builders appeared more bullish. Some, including \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  , have said July's market measures may encourage efforts to do more work abroad. Daiwa yesterday called bids "relatively upbeat" and that they suggest many developers still have a constructive outlook on the residential market. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT - The second straight drop in German manufacturing orders doesn't bode well for Germany's industrial production and future growth. Incoming orders "again clearly disappointed expectations," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  economist \uc2 Ralph Solveen\uc2 . Total manufacturing orders fell 0.9% on the month in July, exacerbating June's 3.9% drop, data from the statistics body show. "Production is likely to fall further in the coming months...and the German economy as a whole is likely to grow only moderately," Mr. Solveen says. (nina.adam@wsj.com, @Nina_Adam_)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT - \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  is expected to leave rates on hold at -0.5% when it announces a decision at 0830 GMT. This leaves focus on its forecasts for future rate rises. \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  to "hint at a lower probability of a rate hike this year." This would likely cause the Swedish krona to fall, but not by much given this is broadly what the market is pricing. "We shouldn't expect a sharp reaction in EUR/SEK: the cross should move higher but 10.70 should not be in danger." However, the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  sticking to its current rate path would be a "hawkish signal" which could send EUR/SEK down toward 10.40, it says. EUR/SEK last up 0.1% at 10.5363. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT - Germany's economy will grow slightly better than previously forecast this year and next due to strong private consumption and favorable labor market conditions, the \uc2 Ifo institute\uc2  says. The Munich-based institute raises its growth forecast by one percentage point to 1.9% for both this year and next after growth of 2.5% in 2017. "We have currently a strong economy in Germany," says Ifo. Low interest rates help boost investments and companies' full order books point to higher production, it says. Germany's export industry should benefit from the strong global economy, although Ifo said it has based its growth scenario on the assumption of a truce in the trade dispute between the U.S. and Europe and an orderly exit of the U.K. from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  . (andrea.thomas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT - The Pacific island nation of Nauru has signed a $79.6 million deal to improve its main port with the support of the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  , the UN 's Green Climate Fund and Australia. The deal comes as the ADB and Western nations-- including Japan, the US and Australia--try to set up alternative infrastructure-funding pipelines to small South Pacific nations as an alternative to concessional loans from China. Under the deal signed today, Nauru's 111-year-old Aiwo boat harbor will be redeveloped. ADB is providing a $21.3 million grant while the fund will contribute $26.9 million and Australia US$14.1 million. Nauru will provide $17.3 million. (rob.taylor@wsj.com; @WSJRobTaylor)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT - The rally in Italian government bonds this week, which has been driving yields lower, has been pushing German Bund yields higher, indicating less appetite for safe havens. German Bund yields may come under more upward pressure on Thursday with heavy bond supply in Spain and France looming. "Combined with duration-heavy supply this implies more pressure on 10-year Bunds but 0.40% should emerge as a strong resistance again," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist Michael Leister. Spain will offer up to EUR5.75 billion in nominal and inflation-linked bonds, while France will auction up to EUR8.5 billion long- and ultra-long nominal bonds. The 10-year Bund yield trades at 0.38%, down 0.3 basis points, according to Tradeweb.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT - Declines in Japanese stocks again were less than a number of other margins in the region today, even as the yen regained some lost ground during Asian trading and a fresh natural disaster hit the country. The Nikkei fell 0.4% to 22487.94, a 5th-straight drop and first such slide since last January. Insurers fared worst as a big earthquake in Hokkaido this morning added to Tuesday's typhoon Jebi, with declines of 2-3%. The dollar is around Y111.31, versus Y111.53 late Wednesday in New York, and 10-year JGB yields eased a half-basis point to 0.105%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT - Switzerland's economy also had a pretty gangbusters 2Q, with GDP rising 0.7% on a quarterly basis and 3.4% year-over-year. The latter was helped in part by upward revisions to previous quarters' results. Meanwhile, the economic affairs department says, "This expansion was largely supported by manufacturing, which has been experiencing a remarkable upturn since spring of 2017. Exports of goods increased accordingly." The agency added that trade tensions have "had hardly any impact." (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT - It's been a rough 20 hours for bitcoin following reports that \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has dropped plans to open a trading desk for cryptocurrencies. Such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a leading investment bank would have given a level of credibility to what some have considered the Wild West of financial markets. After hovering near $7,400 in late-morning European trading Wednesday, bitcoin fell to $7,000 in an hour, according to CoinDesk. It trended slightly lower right through the U.S. trading day before falling $600 to $6,300 within an hour as Asian markets opened, putting it back near last month's lows. Bitcoin has since ticked up to $6,430. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0505 GMT - India's finance minister says the rupee's weakness has been the result of global turmoil and that the country will overcome the situation due to its economic strength. The rupee has been one of the worst performers in Asia this year against the dollar, falling 12%. Arun Jaitley cited dollars moving back to the US and oil's gains as weighing on the rupee. And though "we are not in the trade war," he said that when a neighbor devalues its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --an apparent reference to China--it also impacts the rupee. But Jaitley doesn't see a need for the world's fastest-growing emerging-market economy to have a knee-jerk reaction to the rupee's moves. Analysts have said \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slowed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the past few weeks. The dollar is at INR71.89. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 06, 2018 03:34 ET (07:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180906ee96000de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
