\par\par \b \uc2 India FinMin: Economic Growth to Support the Rupee -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2018\par 07:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0505 GMT - India's finance minister says the rupee's weakness has been the result of global turmoil and that the country will overcome the situation due to its economic strength. The rupee has been one of the worst performers in Asia this year against the dollar, falling 12%. Arun Jaitley cited dollars moving back to the US and oil's gains as weighing on the rupee. And though "we are not in the trade war," he said that when a neighbor devalues its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --an apparent reference to China--it also impacts the rupee. But Jaitley doesn't see a need for the world's fastest-growing emerging-market economy to have a knee-jerk reaction to the rupee's moves. Analysts have said \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slowed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the past few weeks. The dollar is at INR71.89. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT - The \uc2 Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it remains "strongly committed" to meeting the government's 2-4% inflation target and will reassess the medium-term price path after inflation rose a more-than-expected 6.4% from a year ago in August. That was the most in 9 1/2 years. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets in 3 weeks, and the comment is a likely indication that a 4th-straight rate hike is in the offing. The \uc2 BSP\uc2  added it will consider the inflation data "with increased vigilance," it adds. Meanwhile, Philippine stocks are slumping anew today, with the PSEi index down 2.3% adding to yesterday's 1.6% drop. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0343 GMT - ANZ remains positive on spending in China despite trade tensions with the US as services consumption builds. The comment comes as the investment bank says the slowdown seen in July retail sales may have been caused by ongoing mainland stock-market weakness and increasing mortgage burdens. But ANZ adds that the retail report doesn't "represent total household consumption in China." For one, the bank contends that China's retail-sales growth would be 1-2 percentage points higher if online activity was included. ANZ says solid momentum for services consumption will help offset sluggish retail sales. August data should be released next week. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT - This calendar year to date, Australia's trade surplus with China is annualized at A$42 billion, and exceeds the 2013 record of A$39 billion, followed by Japan at A$30 billion, notes \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  . LNG exports have tripled in two years and are approaching A$40 billion. In the absence of trade with these top two trading partners, Australia's current account deficit this year would be closer to -6.5% of GDP rather than a low -2.7% of GDP, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  . (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT - Australian exports have been helping boost the country's economic growth, but is that about to change? \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says July's 1% drop in outbound shipments is set to continue through year-end given the bulk of Australia's major LNG, iron-ore and coal \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are approaching output capacity. The investment bank sees net exports being neutral to GDP by New Year's. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT - The decline to 1% for Australia's household-savings rate in 2Q shows last quarter's GDP jump was "reliant on an incredible and unsustainable drop" in the ratio, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  . The savings rate hit levels last seen in 2007, and the investment bank sees consumption and economic growth moderating from here. To \uc2 UBS\uc2  , that means no interest-rate hike until 2020. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  was seen selling the dollar in the spot market at IDR-14,890-900, helping the rupiah tick up to IDR14,880/dollar from IDR14,920 yesterday, says a dealer with a foreign bank. That as overall sentiment on the greenback has softened a bit. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also selling yesterday to help support the rupiah, which earlier this week hit 20-year lows against the dollar. Meanwhile, Indonesian stocks have opened with slight declines after logging their biggest drop in 22 months yesterday. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT - The \uc2 Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase rates in 4Q18 to manage inflation expectations and control capital outflow risks if a trade war doesn't materialize, says \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  . Food inflation is expected to rise after late August's rainstorms in central and southern Taiwan with the softer NT dollar adding to overall inflation, says the broker. "The key downside risk, in our view, is prolonged and escalated trade tension beyond U.S. mid-term elections," which may induce \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to delay any tightening on concerns over disrupting growth, the broker says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT - Though Australia's trade surplus narrowed in July, even having one is still relatively new in the country's economic history and reflect export growth in commodities and services like education. It follows an investment boom that peaked early this decade. A weaker Aussie dollar is helping to promote the competitiveness of the country's goods and services. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT - China guided the yuan 0.1% stronger against the dollar today. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  fixed the midpoint for daily trading at CNY6.8217, compared with CNY6.8266 yesterday and the onshore close of CNY6.8381. The pair has been around current levels for nearly 2 weeks. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT - \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  could stay on the sidelines for the foreseeable future and well into 2019 as the lack of inflation and "subpar" GDP gives no cause to move rates higher, says Stephen Innes, head of trading in Asia for Oanda. The next shift for the \uc2 BNM\uc2  may even be a rate cut towards the end of 2019 given the economic headwinds from a reduction in infrastructure spending, and more austerity measures aimed to reduce the burgeoning budget deficit, he says. Innes now sees the USD/MYR pair at 4.20 by year-end versus his earlier forecast of MYR4.05 as "traders position for a dovish BNM" in 2019. The USD/MYR is down 0.1% at 4.15. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT - Malaysia's evening announcement of a standardized minimum wage for private-sector employees in the new year shouldn't hit businesses much, analysts say. There will be a 5% increase of levels in peninsula Malaysia and 14% for the Borneo states. All areas will have a monthly minimum wage of MYR1,050 ($253). TA Securities views the short-term costs to businesses, if any, to be minimal and well-contained as many are already paying at the higher rate. Meanwhile, it could have a positive impact on the Malaysian economy via higher consumer spending. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 06, 2018 01:05 ET (05:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180906ee9600061\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
