\par\par \b \uc2 India FinMin: Economic Growth to Support the Rupee -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 172 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2018\par 07:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0505 GMT - India's finance minister says the rupee's weakness has been the result of global turmoil and that the country will overcome the situation due to its economic strength. The rupee has been one of the worst performers in Asia this year against the dollar, falling 12%. Arun Jaitley cited dollars moving back to the US and oil's gains as weighing on the rupee. And though "we are not in the trade war," he said that when a neighbor devalues its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --an apparent reference to China--it also impacts the rupee. But Jaitley doesn't see a need for the world's fastest-growing emerging-market economy to have a knee-jerk reaction to the rupee's moves. Analysts have said \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slowed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the past few weeks. The dollar is at INR71.89. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 06, 2018 01:05 ET (05:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180906ee960008u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
