\par\par \b \uc2 India's 'Strength' May Instead Be a Snapback -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2018\par 08:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0640 GMT - The impressive bounce in recent economic data is widely perceived as a sign of a full-fledged recovery. But Edelweiss Securities doesn't think so, contending that much of the improvement is a natural reversal of the impacts from demonetization and GST's implementation. While benefits of the reforms will trickle in, the broker says that slowing global trade, high oil prices and tightening domestic liquidity pose near-term challenges. It adds the rupee's weakness may not offer much tailwind. However, any sharp fall in crude prices and a dovish turn by the Fed could dramatically improve India's balance-of-payment and liquidity situations, says \uc2 Edelweiss\uc2 . (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT - Japanese stocks held up better than others in the region, perhaps helped some by some overnight yen softness. As the dollar remained around Y111.50, versus Y111.15 at the end of Tuesday's stock trading, the Nikkei fell 0.5% to 22580.83, logging a 4th-straight drop. Cosmetics makers lead falls as concerns grow about Typhoon Jebi's impact on travel demand. \uc2 Kose\uc2  slid 6.7% and \uc2 Shiseido\uc2  lost 4.2%. Meanwhile, 10-year JGB yields fell a half-basis point to 0.11%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT - After slower manufacturing growth last month, India's service-sector \uc2 PMI\uc2  today showed a pullback from 21-month highs. The Nikkei/Markit dropped to 51.5 in August from 54.2, the least since May. Although orders increased, Markit says profit margins came under pressure on higher costs and businesses refraining from passing them on fully to customers. But it was the 6th-straight reading in expansion territory (above 50), the longest streak in 2 years. Further, businesses sentiment improved, Markit says, indicating expectations of a broader strengthening in demand. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0617 GMT - Though Malaysian exports rose more than expected in July at 9.4% from a year earlier, \uc2 Nomura\uc2  says the reading affirms its view that GDP growth will remain pressured in 2H following 2Q's weaker-than-expected 4.5% increase. The investment bank's 2018 forecast is 4.7%, versus the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5% view. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT - As the record lows continue for the rupee, putting the year's drop versus the dollar at 12%, speculative dollar-buying in India is helping extend the move, says Rushabh Maru at \uc2 Anand Rathi\uc2  Shares and Stock Brokers. That as investors have continued to fret the rebound in oil prices the past few weeks, which could aggravate India's current-account deficit. The dollar got as high as INR71.79. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT - Indonesia's 5-year CDS, which measure the cost of insuring government debt against potential defaults--have eased so far today to 1.39% of coverage amounts from 1.42% yesterday. But the shorter end continues to prices rise, with 2-year CDS up to 0.59% from 0.57%. India's proxy, the 5-year CDS of the \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  is up nearly a basis point at 1.23%, according to \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  . The CDS action comes amid a relatively stable rupiah in Asian trading and Indonesia's JSX stock benchmark slumping 3.3% as of the midday break. On Aug. 13, the index closed down 3.6% amid the initial plunge in the Turkish lira. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT - Economists in a Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey see the greenback fetching S$1.37 at year-end, not far off of 2018's high. After trading below S$1.32 through 1Q amid broad US dollar weakness, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadily risen since, getting to S$1.38 last month before a modest pullback as the greenback widely cooled. It's currently around S$1.3760.(gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT - Hong Kong's private sector saw continued contraction in August per Nikkei's \uc2 PMI\uc2  reading. It came in at 48.5, versus July's 48.2, as output and new orders declined further last month amid softer client demand, says index compiler \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  . That as business confidence hit 20-month lows with US-China trade frictions "a key concern." The greenback's strength was an added reason cited for caution. The report said headline \uc2 PMI\uc2  is consistent with GDP growth of around 2% for 3Q. But the economy has been growing much stronger than that, including 2Q's 3.5%, as retail sales logged double-digit growth in 1H before slowing some in July. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT - Although the Indian rupee touched all-time lows versus the dollar to become Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, it remains overvalued, says ANZ. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  's real effective exchange rate measures are still above their long-term average, an indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still on the expensive side, the bank says. Its internal model sees the USD/INR at around 73 (no timeline) with the rupee already weakening past ANZ's year-end forecast of 71.5. Meanwhile, the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restricted as liquidity is tight and as \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance of payments position deteriorates further.(kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT - The unexpectedly high 6.4% increase in Philippine CPI last month may trigger further interest-rate hikes. Price growth is at 9 1/2-year highs and more than double the midpoint of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range. It's risen rates at the last 3 meetings, but inflation hasn't stopped accelerating yet amid soaring prices of food and nonalcoholic beverages. That as alcohol and tobacco prices have jumped because of higher taxes. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT - Weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of India and Indonesia are adding to the woes of the countries' local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. For 10-year Indian government debt, yields yesterday closed above 8% for the first time since December 2014 amid worries of further interest-rate hikes. That debt finished at 8.06%, while at above 8.3% Indonesia's 10-year bond yields are at levels last seen in November 2016. Five-year CDS costs for Indonesia also jumped yesterday. In general, traders expect more pain if the broad emerging-market weakness persists. Investors seem to be betting so, with stock indexes in Indonesia and the Philippines falling nearly 2% this morning. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT - Australia's 2Q GDP report showing the household-savings rate at an 11-year low of 1% shows consumers continuing to tap their nest eggs in the face of scant wage growth, high debt, falling house prices and rising living costs. It raises the question of how long consumers can keep it up, key because they helped fuel last quarter's stronger-than-expected economic growth. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 05, 2018 02:40 ET (06:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180905ee950008b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
