\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Seen Stabilizing Around Here -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2018\par 06:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0417 GMT - Economists in a Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey see the greenback fetching S$1.37 at year-end, not far off of 2018's high. After trading below S$1.32 through 1Q amid broad US dollar weakness, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadily risen since, getting to S$1.38 last month before a modest pullback as the greenback widely cooled. It's currently around S$1.3760.(gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT - Hong Kong's private sector saw continued contraction in August per Nikkei's \uc2 PMI\uc2  reading. It came in at 48.5, versus July's 48.2, as output and new orders declined further last month amid softer client demand, says index compiler \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  . That as business confidence hit 20-month lows with US-China trade frictions "a key concern." The greenback's strength was an added reason cited for caution. The report said headline \uc2 PMI\uc2  is consistent with GDP growth of around 2% for 3Q. But the economy has been growing much stronger than that, including 2Q's 3.5%, as retail sales logged double-digit growth in 1H before slowing some in July. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT - Although the Indian rupee touched all-time lows versus the dollar to become Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, it remains overvalued, says ANZ. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  's real effective exchange rate measures are still above their long-term average, an indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still on the expensive side, the bank says. Its internal model sees the USD/INR at around 73 (no timeline) with the rupee already weakening past ANZ's year-end forecast of 71.5. Meanwhile, the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restricted as liquidity is tight and as \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance of payments position deteriorates further.(kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT - The unexpectedly high 6.4% increase in Philippine CPI last month may trigger further interest-rate hikes. Price growth is at 9 1/2-year highs and more than double the midpoint of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range. It's risen rates at the last 3 meetings, but inflation hasn't stopped accelerating yet amid soaring prices of food and nonalcoholic beverages. That as alcohol and tobacco prices have jumped because of higher taxes. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT - Weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of India and Indonesia are adding to the woes of the countries' local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. For 10-year Indian government debt, yields yesterday closed above 8% for the first time since December 2014 amid worries of further interest-rate hikes. That debt finished at 8.06%, while at above 8.3% Indonesia's 10-year bond yields are at levels last seen in November 2016. Five-year CDS costs for Indonesia also jumped yesterday. In general, traders expect more pain if the broad emerging-market weakness persists. Investors seem to be betting so, with stock indexes in Indonesia and the Philippines falling nearly 2% this morning. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT - Australia's 2Q GDP report showing the household-savings rate at an 11-year low of 1% shows consumers continuing to tap their nest eggs in the face of scant wage growth, high debt, falling house prices and rising living costs. It raises the question of how long consumers can keep it up, key because they helped fuel last quarter's stronger-than-expected economic growth. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT - There's a strong chance the board of \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a fairly robust rundown on today's upbeat 2Q GDP data at yesterday's policy meeting. It helps explains the unaltered optimism of RBA Gov. Lowe's commentary. But he also said Tuesday that a rate hike is still some way off. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  will wait for strong growth to lower unemployment further and for wages and inflation to rise before starting to tighten. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT - The bigger-than-expected 2Q economic growth in Australia validates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's optimism for now. But questions will be asked about whether the 3.4% increase from a year earlier can be sustained, especially with the household-savings ratio at its lowest level since 2007. The Australian dollar jumped to session highs on the report, but equities continued morning weakness are at fresh session lows, with the ASX 200 down 0.8%. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT - China guides the yuan 0.1% weaker against the dollar, with the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  fixing the midpoint for the dollar's trading range at CNY6.8266. That compares with CNY6.8183 yesterday and the day's onshore close of CNY6.8290. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0041 GMT - Singapore's private-sector economy slowed further in August, with both output and new orders easing, according to an alternative \uc2 PMI\uc2  reading. The sharp fall in the Nikkei Singapore PMI--to 51.1 from July's 53--contrasts with a rebound in the official reading released Monday. It's likely an indication of uneven growth in different sectors. Most economists predict a slower 2H as Singapore's economy levels off from a strong start to 2018. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT - Malaysian export growth to the US will slow to 3-5% this year from 2017's 10% increase amid tariff hikes earlier in 2018 on products like washer machines, solar panels and metals, predicts MIDF Research. The firm estimates that every 1% drop in demand from the US will reduce Malaysia's economic output by 0.86%. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT - The market is trying to understand if China is genuine in its guidance for a stable yuan, or whether the authorities will offset trade tariffs with greater purchasing power for U.S. importers by giving them a stronger dollar, says Pepperstone head of research Chris Weston. Should the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  take the view that they need to offset a full suite of 25% tariffs, then the yuan is going lower, he says. "Concerns of capital outflows and equity drawdowns will ramp back up again and this is the balancing act the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  need to face," Weston says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 05, 2018 00:17 ET (04:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180905ee95002hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
