\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,094 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2018\par 05:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Equities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT - Although the Indian rupee touched all-time lows versus the dollar to become Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, it remains overvalued, says ANZ. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  's real effective exchange rate measures are still above their long-term average, an indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still on the expensive side, the bank says. Its internal model sees the USD/INR at around 73 (no timeline) with the rupee already weakening past ANZ's year-end forecast of 71.5. Meanwhile, the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is restricted as liquidity is tight and as \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance of payments position deteriorates further.(kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT - Malaysian construction firm Pesona Metro winning a MYR264.3 million ($63.8 million) contract from TA Global to build a serviced apartment in Petaling Jaya has gotten investors enthused. Shares have jumped 4.4% to 5-month highs, reaching MYR0.355 and putting the week's bounce at 9.2%. \uc2 Hong Leong\uc2 's target climbs 14% to MYR0.32 as the investment bank remains cautious on the job-flow outlook amid the new government's review on mega projects. Pesona shares have dropped 21% this year. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT - Broad declines again this morning in Asia Pacific with indexes in just New Zealand and Taiwan barely up. Conversely, benchmarks in Indonesia and the Philippines are down nearly 2% as emerging-market worries persist. The dollar edged above IDR15,000 overnight, though the rupiah has rebounded a bit in Asian trading. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hit 20-year lows this week, the Philippine peso is at mid-2000 levels. Hong Kong stocks are another soft point, with tech names pressuring the market there. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT - Though 2Q earnings largely disappointed in AmBank Research's view at Malaysian oil-and-gas companies, the broker remains upbeat on the sector with crude prices stabilizing above $75/barrel and asset-utilization rates starting to improve. The optimism comes despite national oil company \uc2 Petronas\uc2 ' cautious capital-spending plans. But AmBank thinks that charter rates have bottomed out even with an absence of any upward trajectory at this juncture. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT - The growth potential still enormous in China's natural-gas market, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  , with consumption projected to triple by 2030 as the country seeks out cleaner fuels. The investment bank starts coverage at buy on \uc2 ENN\uc2  and China Gas , versus neutral for CR Gas. The trio is up 16-32% this year in Hong Kong, easily outpacing the broader market. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT - Malaysian media firms' 1H results were largely disappointing despite big events like May's election and the June start to the World Cup, says Affin Hwang. Though revenue increased 2.4% from a year earlier, driven mainly by non-core traditional media businesses, the sector fell into the red. That as ad momentum weakened post-election as caution emerged amid the change in government. Affin Hwang believes structural changes in the industry will keep the operating environment very challenging for print and TV as the rest of the year is lacks notable events. The broker maintains underweight on the media sector. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT - The bigger-than-expected 2Q economic growth in Australia validates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's optimism for now. But questions will be asked about whether the 3.4% increase from a year earlier can be sustained, especially with the household-savings ratio at its lowest level since 2007. The Australian dollar jumped to session highs on the report, but equities continued morning weakness are at fresh session lows, with the ASX 200 down 0.8%. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT - Following a rebound yesterday, Hong Kong stocks are underwater again with others in the region, with tech names leading the pressure. The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  is down 0.7% at 27767 with Unicom and \uc2 China Telecom\uc2  both down about 2% after jumping yesterday on the potential of the state-controlled wireless firms merging. Meanwhile, internet giant and index heavyweight Tencent is down 1.8%, reversing yesterday's rebound. The China Enterprises Index drops 0.5%. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT - Chinese stocks have opened lower along with most of the region as the domestic market falls anew after after an up to a week long losing streak ended yesterday. The Shanghai Composite is down 0.3% and others are similarly lower. In the big-cap CSI 300, the real-estate sector is 0.9% lower. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT - Singapore stocks are also solidly lower in early trading as the Straits Times Index returns to key support. The benchmark is down 0.4% at 3198 amid regional declines, with banks lagging as is the case in neighboring Malaysia. Meanwhile, \uc2 Keppel\uc2  has dropped 0.9% though fellow conglomerate \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  is up 0.4%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT - Malaysian stocks have slid strongly to start trading amid fresh regional declines. The Kuala Lumpur Composite, which lagged a number of benchmarks yesterday in logging a 4th-straight drop, is down a further 0.5% at 1803 ahead of today's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy statement. Lenders are lagging, with AMMB down 1.5%. Telecom stocks are down similarly as that sector remains weak. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT - Chipset maker \uc2 PC Partner Group\uc2  's more than 45% decline this year "opens another opportunity to add to a severely misunderstood name at a very attractive valuation," says Nicolas Van Broekhoven, writing on the Smartkarma research platform. Its stock price has dropped to HK$5.30 from a post-earnings spike to HK$7.20 when 1H net profit surged more than seven-fold on-year on a more than 86% rise in revenue, he says. That prices PC Partner at 3.75 times trailing earnings. "Stocks don't normally trade at 3X P/E, 11% dividend yield and over 29%+ FCF (free cash flow) yield," says Broekhoven. Concerns over inventory building up ahead of \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  's new chip launch, growth hurting from Chinese gaming approvals and 2018 earnings peaking are "way too pessimistic," he says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 04, 2018 23:06 ET (03:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180905ee95001tn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
