\par\par \b \uc2 Lira Rises, But Savings Measures Won't be Enough, Says ING -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,157 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2018\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0800 GMT - The lira rises after Turkey announced measures designed to encourage local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  saving, cutting a tax on lira deposits and increasing a tax on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits. Having initially jumped almost 4% against the dollar, the lira soon pares gains, however, with USD/TRY last down 0.9% at 6.5822. ING says the measures are "not enough to credibly stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness" and it expects any lira gains to be "short-lived." The scale of the tax measures aren't "aggressive enough" given the scale of moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What's really needed to lift the lira are "orthodox" measures, such as interest rate increases to "change market perception about the lira," ING says. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT - Indonesian inflation is predicted to have ticked up to 3.3% this month from July's 3.2% amid the rupiah's ongoing declines, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by WSJ. On-month, prices of goods and services are seen to have risen 0.06%, versus July's 0.3%. The data are due Monday. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT - Indian retail-fuel prices are at record highs amid the rupee's woes. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says while fuel may not have a major impact on inflation or change the course of monetary policy, it does "create a political headache" for the ruling BJP government ahead of elections next year. The firm does anticipate prices easing near-term as new supply comes online. Meanwhile, CapEcon sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping its tightening bias regardless of how much fuel may move. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the rupee as the record lows continue, says Rushabh Maru at \uc2 Anand Rathi\uc2  Shares and Stock Brokers. He notes the \uc2 RBI\uc2  has been stepping in very selectively to contain volatility. The dollar briefly topped INR71 and is currently around INR70.90. The rupee has fallen 11% this year amid pressures from rising oil prices and broader weakness for emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Fiscal-deficit and GDP data due later today will provide further direction for the rupee. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT - As the market awaits \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  ' scheduled review of Italy's credit rating later on Friday, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategists trade into the expectations. "We are brave and suggest tactical longs in [Italian government bonds] BTPs ahead of tonight's Fitch review," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist say. They add that the overall tone is mixed with expectations of higher debt and more difficult reforms in Italy giving rise to a potentially negative outlook. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  currently rates Italy at 'BBB' with stable outlook. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT - Italy lines up for a scheduled credit-rating review by \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  later on Friday but \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's analysts say they don't see a downgrade as likely, which "should be greeted with relief." However, negative newsflow in Italy is picking up and with budget deadlines approaching, "we are seeing more evidence of dissension within the [Italian governing] coalition," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's analysts say. Against this backdrop, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  continues to largely underweigh Italian government bonds, BTPs. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  currently rates Italy at 'BBB' with stable outlook. At 0642 GMT Italy's 10-year BTP is trading at a yield of 3.22%, up 1.4 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT - Trade tensions are once again at the forefront, pushing 10-year German Bund yields back below 0.40% as investors seek safe havens. "Trade issues are likely to reclaim the spotlight today, even as there is a full slate of data, including what we expect to be this year's peak in euro area inflation at 2.1%," say \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's analysts. There is no government bond supply in the eurozone on Friday but France and Spain announce the details of their upcoming bond auctions to be held on Sept. 6. The 10-year German Bund yield is trading at 0.35%, up 0.4% basis points. Yields move inversely to prices. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT - After clawing its way back to positive territory this afternoon, the Nikkei slipped into the close and saw its 8-day winning streak end with a 4.35-point drop to 22865.15. But Japanese stock benchmark still rose 1.4% this month, the most since January. Overnight yen strength weighted today, with financials the biggest decliners. The dollar remains just below Y111 and the risk-off mood amid trade worries helped push 10-year JGB yields down a half-basis point to 0.1%. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0559 GMT - \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  's split rate decision yesterday and policy comments are signaling the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "left the door open" to a hike if policymakers see no further significant downside risks, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . The BoK has hinted at lifting rates again since November's hike, but weak job growth and subdued inflation kept it sidelined. On Thursday, a dissenter again called for tighter policy while \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  's statement took note of a growing need to fend off a possible spread of emerging-market volatility from reaching the country's shores. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  sees a 60% probability of a quarter-point rate hike in November. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com; @kwanwoo)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT - Australia is still a very-long way from a recession or financial crisis, but this week's news that \uc2 Westpac\uc2  is hiking mortgage rates has raised the risk a bit--especially if other major banks follow. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says higher rates, still-falling home prices and tighter credit conditions aren't a good mix. But it sees the most-likely result of Australia's debt binge being just a prolonged period of subdued economic growth. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT - Despite the August uptick in manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2 , \uc2 Nomura\uc2  says that doesn't mean China's economic growth is rebounding. The investment bank notes that the reading's increase was riven by increased production while new orders slowed. Activity could be getting frontloaded as the US could put tariffs on another $200 billion of Chinese exports, it adds. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT - Though a number of firms have been warning of a potential acceleration of Aussie home-price declines, July credit data show they could be drawing to a close. Credit for housing investors rose 0.1% from June. While just a single datapoint, sustained improvement for that segment would be a lead indicator of stabilization in housing prices, says ANZ. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 31, 2018 04:00 ET (08:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180831ee8v000o8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
