\par\par \b \uc2 Press Release: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  : India Sovereign Credit Risk from Rupee Decline Is Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 2,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2018\par 05:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 -Hong Kong/Singapore/Mumbai-19 August 2018: A recent sharp sell-off in the rupee illustrates India's sensitivity to shifts in market sentiment towards emerging markets, and suggests there could be further bouts of pressure as global monetary tightening progresses, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. However, the impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness on India's sovereign credit profile is likely to be limited by relatively strong external finances, especially the low level of external debt. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation could nevertheless add to existing pressures in the corporate and banking sectors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The rupee has been among the emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  affected by pressures from global monetary tightening and, more recently, contagion from the Turkey crisis. It has depreciated by around 9% against the US dollar since the start of 2018, making it the worst-performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia (see chart). Idiosyncratic factors have also contributed, such as the widening of the trade deficit in July 2018 to its largest gap since May 2013. Net portfolio outflows through mid-August have totalled USD5.5 billion for the year, mostly in bonds, compared with inflows of USD27.9 billion over the same period in 2017. Foreign direct investment inflows have also weakened and no longer cover the current account deficit - in other words, India's basic balance has turned negative.\par \par \uc2 <iframe src=" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/e3fde352-d774-4a02-84fe-fc532b664c4f?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://e.infogram.com/e3fde352-d774-4a02-84fe-fc532b664c4f?src=embed }}}\uc2 " title="Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " width="550" height="594" scrolling="no" frameborder="0" style="border:none;" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe>\par \par \uc2 However, India's vulnerability to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk and capital outflows is unlikely to translate into significant pressure on the sovereign credit profile or pose external financing risks. The current account deficit has widened as global oil prices have risen, but at 1.9% of GDP in 1Q18, up from 1.6% in 2017, was still well below the 5% of GDP recorded around the time of the 2013 Taper Tantrum. We expect the full-year current account deficit to remain below 3.0% of GDP in the fiscal year ending March 2019. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have declined by USD24 billion since mid-April 2018, but still cover 7.2 months of current account payments, compared with the 'BBB' median of 6.6, providing a buffer should the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 feel it necessary to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a larger scale.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has relatively low foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt. Only around 7% of government debt is denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BBB median: 38%), while total foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  external debt, including the private sector, is equivalent to just 13% of GDP, which is one of the lowest among major emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures triggering a sudden spike in domestic borrowing costs is mitigated by the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's relatively narrow focus on its inflation objective, as opposed to countering external pressures. This is in contrast to the approach in Indonesia, for example, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has responded to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures with aggressive interest rate increases. External pressures may be enough to prompt another 25bp hike this year, but dramatic moves by the \uc2 RBI\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear unlikely in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view.\par \par \uc2 India's corporate sector is less dependent on foreign borrowing than in most other emerging markets, but hedging practices are limited, particularly at companies lower down the credit scale. The most vulnerable companies are likely to be those with significant foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, and which operate in sectors with significant imported/commodity inputs and revenue denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - such as the airline, telecom, auto, chemical and fertiliser sectors. Most \uc2 Fitch\uc2 -rated corporates are unlikely to be significantly affected, as those with foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt tend to have a natural hedge from export earnings, as in the case of commodity producers.\par \par \uc2 The banking sector has relatively low direct foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, with foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans accounting for just 10.9% of total loans in March 2018, as per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's estimate, compared with 12.1% a year earlier. However, domestic-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending to corporates with unhedged foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowings could face increased repayment risk. Moreover, Indian banks are already struggling with asset-quality problems and have thin loss-absorption buffers to cope with further stress.\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Thomas Rookmaaker\par \par \uc2 - Director\par \par \uc2 - Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 - +852 2263 9891\par \par \uc2 - Fitch (Hong Kong) Limited\par \par \uc2 - 19/F Man Yee Building\par \par \uc2 - 68 Des Voeux Road Central\par \par \uc2 - Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 Muralidharan R\par \par \uc2 - Director\par \par \uc2 - Corporates\par \par \uc2 - +65 6796 7236\par \par \uc2 Saswata Guha\par \par \uc2 - Director\par \par \uc2 - Financial Institutions\par \par \uc2 - +91 22 4000 1741\par \par \uc2 Dan Martin\par \par \uc2 - Senior Analyst\par \par \uc2 - Fitch Wire\par \par \uc2 - +65 6796 7232\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Leslie Tan, Singapore, Tel: +65 6796 7234 , Email: leslie.tan@fitchratings.com; Wai-Lun Wan, Hong Kong, Tel: +852 2263 9935 , Email: wailun.wan@fitchratings.com.\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: HTTPS://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS. IN ADDITION, RATING DEFINITIONS AND THE TERMS OF USE OF SUCH RATINGS ARE AVAILABLE ON THE AGENCY'S PUBLIC WEB SITE AT WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM. PUBLISHED RATINGS, CRITERIA, AND METHODOLOGIES ARE AVAILABLE FROM THIS SITE AT ALL TIMES. FITCH'S CODE OF CONDUCT, CONFIDENTIALITY, CONFLICTS OF INTEREST, AFFILIATE FIREWALL, COMPLIANCE, AND OTHER RELEVANT POLICIES AND PROCEDURES ARE ALSO AVAILABLE FROM THE CODE OF CONDUCT SECTION OF THIS SITE. DIRECTORS AND SHAREHOLDERS R