\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Drops as Lira Lunacy Sends Investors Rushing for the Exits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Manju Dalal in Singapore and Debiprasad Nayak in Mumbai \par \uc2 1,154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2018\par 10:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee fell to an all-time low against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday as foreign investors kept selling emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spooked by the collapse of the Turkish lira.\par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to an intraday record low of 70.08 in early trade, retaining its position as one of Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the rupee could fall as low as 70.60 in the next few sessions.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been battered this week after the Turkish lira collapsed Monday. Moves by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have failed to calm investors.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has fallen 9.5% in 2018. Among other major emerging markets, only Brazil, Russia and South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have weakened more.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in rupee was not in isolation, rather a part of the broader spelldown in emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 Radhika Rao\uc2  , economist at \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Analysts and brokers said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  has been trying to defend the 70 level by selling U.S. dollars. The \uc2 RBI\uc2  said it doesn't comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "When there is a global crisis, you will not be able to save," said \uc2 Kishore Narne\uc2  , head of commodity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Motilal Oswal Financial Services Ltd\uc2  . "You will only lose \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\uc2  has sold about $23 billion worth of its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves over the past five months, bringing the total down from a record $426 billion in April.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the global selloff of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some investors are specifically concerned about Indian economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 They say they are worried about India's inflation rate and trade imbalances and add that increased government spending ahead of next year's national elections could blow out its budget deficit.\par \par \uc2 In troubled times, investors tend to trim exposure to economies that run wide current account deficits and rely on foreign fund flows. A country falls into a deficit if it imports more goods and services than it exports.\par \par \uc2 Optimists say the rupee may have reached its bottom. India is the fastest-growing large economy in the world and is less exposed to trade frictions that hurt other countries.\par \par \uc2 "It's gradually falling to where it should be," said Salil Datar, chief executive of Essel Finance VKC \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ltd. "It's now fairly valued."\par \par \uc2 Corinne Abrams contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Manju Dalal at manju.dalal@wsj.com and Debiprasad Nayak at debi.nayak@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 14 Aug 2018 12:52 ET  Indian Rupee Drops as Lira Turmoil Sends Investors Rushing for the Exits\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Manju Dalal in Singapore and Debiprasad Nayak in Mumbai \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collapse of the Turkish lira rippled to India Tuesday, where the rupee fell to an all-time low against the dollar, as investors sold emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to an intraday, record low of 70.08 rupees to the dollar in early trade, retaining its position as one of Asia's worst-performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year. Analysts said the rupee could fall as low as 70.60 in the next few sessions. It later recovered some ground to 69.89.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fall in rupee was not in isolation, rather a part of the broader selldown in emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Radhika Rao\f28 \uc2 \f2  , economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 DBS Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee fell by as much as 1.1% on Monday, its largest single-day fall in five years, and has declined 9.5% in 2018. Among other big emerging markets, only Brazil, Russia and South Africa's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have weakened more. The falling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  complicates the India's economic management, stoking inflation by making imports of critical oil and other raw materials more expensive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been battered this week after the Turkish lira collapsed Friday and Monday. Moves by the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have failed to calm investors. Tuesday, the lira rallied slightly, as did the ruble and the South African rand, though all the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are still far below where they started the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina, already under pressure even before Turkey's woes, raised interest rates 5 percentage points Monday to 45% in an effort to attract capital into its economy, yet its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  failed to rally.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To fight the falling rupee, analysts and brokers said the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, snapping up the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and selling dollars from its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBI\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it doesn't comment on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Market participants think it has been trying to keep the rupee below 70 to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBI\f28 \uc2 \f2  sold about $23 billion worth of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves over the past five months, bringing the total down from a record $426 billion in April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When there is a global crisis, you will not be able to save," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kishore Narne\f28 \uc2 \f2  , head of commodity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Motilal Oswal Financial Services Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "You will only lose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to the global selloff of emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some investors are specifically concerned about Indian economic fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They say they are worried about India's inflation rate and trade imbalances and add that increased government spending ahead of next year's national elections could blow out its budget deficit. A weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exacerbates inflation by making imports more expensive and India was already suffering from higher oil prices this year. India is particularly vulnerable to oil because it imports 80% of its energy needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In troubled times, investors tend to trim exposure to economies that run wide current-account deficits and rely on foreign fund flows. A country falls into a deficit if it imports more goods and services than it exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, India seems to be in a better position than during the taper tantrum in 2013 when it was clubbed with Turkey, Brazil, South Africa and Indonesia as a member of "the fragile five." India's current-account deficit, though rising, is much lower today than the record 4.2% of GDP in 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is now the fastest-growing large economy in the world and is less exposed to trade frictions that hurt other countries. India's exports made up 18.9% of its gross domestic product in 2017 compared with 20.4% in Indonesia and 26% of Russia, according to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Optimists say the rupee may have reached its bottom.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's gradually falling to where it should be," said Salil Datar, chief executive of Essel Finance VKC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Ltd. "It's now fairly valued."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Corinne Abrams contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Manju Dalal at manju.dalal@wsj.com and Debiprasad Nayak at debi.nayak@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 14, 2018 12:52 ET (16:52 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180814ee8e000wx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
