\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  says RBI  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  may not do much to stabilise rupee\b0\par\par\uc2 170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2018\par 11:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not do much to stabilise the rupee as a fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to global factors, a senior finance ministry official said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Subhash Chandra Garg\uc2 , secretary at the department of economic affairs, said the rupee was still performing better than some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the country had sufficient \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 "As \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other economies are also depreciating, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI)\uc2 , by selling dollars in the country, will not help much at this stage for stabilising the rupee," Garg told reporters. "Even if the rupee falls to 80, it will not be a concern provided all other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate."\par \par \uc2 He said the RBI has spent about $23 billion so far to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. (Reporting by Manoj Kumar; Writing by Devidutta Tripathy; Editing by Krishna N. Das) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-14T10:19:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180814ee8e01k3h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
