\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-Select few emerging Asian economies comfortable with Fed hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2018\par 07:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Corrects paragraph 4 to refer to countries and territories)\par \par \uc2 * Countries with current account surplus can afford to hold rates\par \par \uc2 * Investor positioning in debt markets helps surplus countries\par \par \uc2 * Asia more connected to China's economy than in the past\par \par \uc2 By Marius Zaharia\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, June 18 (Reuters) - Higher U.S. rates are rattling many emerging markets in much the same way past tightening cycles did, but the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's hawkishness could also bring cheer for a small group of Asian economies that wouldn't mind seeing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken.\par \par \uc2 Fed rate hikes this year and the prospect of more to come have lifted Treasury yields, prompting investors to switch out of riskier emerging market debt and triggering sharp falls in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Markets in Argentina, Brazil and Turkey took the biggest hits and in Asia, the central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indonesia and the Philippines have raised rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, unlike those countries, which run current account deficits, central banks in external surplus countries and territories such as Thailand, South Korea, Taiwan and, to a lesser extent, Malaysia won't feel compelled to keep up with the Fed's rate hikes, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "I don't see those countries just being forced by the Fed into action because some of them have such enormous surpluses that they would probably be happy to see weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital outflows, at the margin," said \uc2 Frederic Neumann\uc2 , co-head of Asian economic research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from portfolio outflows could help lift below-target inflation and give exporters a shot in the arm at a time of heightened uncertainty over global trade and signs that the Chinese economy may be losing steam.\par \par \uc2 This week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in Thailand and Taiwan are likely to reinforce that outlook, with most economists seeing at most one rate hike in Thailand, Taiwan and South Korea over the next 18 months, compared with the Fed's five or six.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso lost almost 7 percent from January highs and is now trading at its lowest in 12 years. The Indian rupee is near record lows having lost a similar amount, while the Indonesian rupiah is down about 5 percent after two rate hikes and heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the Korean won, the Thai baht and the Taiwan dollar are all down 3 percent from January levels close to multi-year highs while their central banks kept rates steady near record lows.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons why the surplus economies are under less pressure is foreign investor positioning.\par \par \uc2 In deficit countries, investors tend to own shorter-term bonds, which are more liquid and less risky than longer-term debt. In countries with surpluses, investors are more comfortable holding longer-term securities.\par \par \uc2 Since the Fed started raising rates some three years ago, the premium that Indian and Indonesian short-term bonds offers over their U.S. equivalent has dropped by roughly 200 basis points. In the Philippines, the premium has fallen by almost the same amount over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 That differential has narrowed some 200 bps in South Korea, Thailand and Taiwan as well and has even turned negative. However, investors' greater preference for longer-term debt has helped limit downward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressure.\par \par \uc2 Differentials with U.S. 10-year yields shrank 100 bps or less. With the U.S. curve flattening as the economic cycle approaches its peak, that is likely to continue.\par \par \uc2 Another reason why those central banks don't have to track the Fed is that China's rise as a major economic power means that Asia is less synchronised with the U.S. cycle than it was before the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The collapse of the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008 coincided with the moment when developing Asia's trade with China surpassed the sums traded with the United States. Slowing economic momentum in China therefore may have more impact on the rate outlook in Asia than a peaking U.S. economic cycle.\par \par \uc2 "Asian economies -- their synchronisation with the U.S. economy has weakened because of China," said Tan Hui, chief market strategist for Asia at J.P.Morgan Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "It's not easy to see many Asian central banks following the Fed."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Marius Zaharia; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-6-18T06:37:58.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180617ee6h00ku2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
