\par\par \b \uc2 GRAPHIC-Asian economies less vulnerable than in the past\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2018\par 10:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 June 8 (Reuters) - A sharp decline in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year on higher U.S. yields, rising oil prices and a resurgent U.S. dollar has brought attention back to regional economies' external balances and vulnerability in the recent emerging markets sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Although Indonesia, South Korea, India and Malaysia are in the spotlight because their markets are more dependent on foreign investor flows, Asia is comparatively healthier this time than during previous regional crises in 1998 and 2013.\par \par \uc2 The biggest improvements are that the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have been replenished alongside a reduction in current account deficits.\par \par \uc2 However, some signs of weakness remain, and those could prove to be weak links as crude oil prices rise and the risks of a global trade war grow.\par \par \uc2 Net oil importing countries - India, Indonesia and the Philippines - have the biggest current account deficits in the region and are thus vulnerable if they have to attract foreign money to foot bigger oil bills.\par \par \uc2 Most of Asia's central banks have built up their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves over the past three years, leaving them with enough ammunition to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from price volatility. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's conservative guide for reserves adequacy is that they be worth at least three months of imports.\par \par \uc2 All Asian economies have a foreign reserves to import ratio in excess of three, while Malaysia has the lowest ratio of 6.2.\par \par \uc2 But past crises have proved that short-term debt is an equally important consideration for sufficiency of reserves, and countries such as Indonesia, South Korea and Malaysia have a high proportion of foreign portfolio flows, or hot money, that could leave suddenly. Malaysia's external debt to GDP ratio is the highest in the region, followed by Indonesia and Thailand.\par \par \uc2 During times of emerging market crises, a country's fiscal deficit becomes a critical consideration as it influences ratings and foreign investor appetite for that market's assets. Rising crude oil prices are thus a risk as budget deficits in countries such as India and Indonesia could swell.\par \par \uc2 The comfort of stronger economic growth, led by domestic consumption, and of low inflation gives Asia a luxury with monetary policy it never had during previous crises.\par \par \uc2 Countries such as China, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesia have been able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and yet sterilise that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep domestic markets liquid. The macro mix has also allowed some countries to raise rates to put a floor under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to a Reuters poll, \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates two more times this year after hiking twice in May. Economists surveyed also expect India to increase its interest rates in August.\par \par \uc2 India's inflation rate is the highest in the region, followed by the Philippines and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Patturaja Murugaboopathy Editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-6-8T09:52:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180608ee6800di6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
