\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2018\par 10:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  is likely to raise interest rates by 50 basis points at 1100 GMT to "prevent USD/TRY from gravitating back toward 5," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . USD/TRY is up by 0.4% at 4.5704. Other central banks in emerging markets are also either rising interest rates, like the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  , or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, like \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  , to fight the weakness in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT - The Singapore-Kuala Lumpur high-speed-rail project might not be fully off the table, says property firm \uc2 Colliers\uc2 . It thinks Malaysia's new government may be angling to renegotiate the terms to reduce the country's cost share. With Mahathir saying he would only stay PM a year or 2, \uc2 Anwar Ibrahim\uc2  could revisit the plans, adds \uc2 Colliers\uc2 . It also thinks renegotiations may take place with Chinese firms over other big-ticket infrastructure and railway projects planned in Malaysia rather than outright terminations. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 Nomura\uc2  doesn't see a need for further interest-rate moves in India a potential August one unless oil stays above $80/barrel and the government embarks on populist policies. "Another India Rate Hike Looms for August -- Market Talk," published at 0458 GMT, incorrectly said does.\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT - China's robust bond financing, which helped to offset the contraction in shadow credit early this year, slumped in May on rising credit risks, say Macquarie economists. "Now, weak credit growth and rising credit risk are reinforcing each other and forming a vicious cycle." They are watching closely whether the first default for a local-government financing vehicle will happen this year. If it does, the investment bank contends risk premiums could surge and infrastructure investment might be hurt. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT - The September gilt future contract, currently trading at 121.83, remains capped by its declining 50-period moving average at 122.45 on a 30-min chart. Moreover, the intraday RSI stands within its selling area between 50 and 30, which confirms the bearish bias. As a consequence, below the horizontal resistance at 122.55, further weakness is expected towards the horizontal support at 121.60 at first. A break below this threshold would open the way towards the previous overlap at 121.02 and towards the horizontal support at 120.31 in extension. Alternatively, a rebound above the strong horizontal resistance at 122.55 would favor an up move towards the horizontal resistance at 123.17 and towards May 31 top at 123.52 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting this month will likely strengthen the euro, but not too much, says ING. "Our team think the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will want to retain flexibility over the end date for QE until later in the year and thus it is unclear that EUR/USD, especially with a potentially hawkish Fed, has much more immediate upside above the 1.1850 area," ING says. Investors would still look for clues and hints on when that due date may be. EUR/USD is up by 0.4% at 1.1822. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT - \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Peter Praet\uc2 's comments Wednesday "could indeed be the prelude to a decision on QE in June," ING's rates strategists say. But they add that ING's economists think the \uc2 ECB\uc2  "might not easily give away flexibility and room for manoeuvre on QE in a situation where downside risks to the economic outlook have increased and political risks could easily re-emerge." ING's base case remains, "albeit narrowly," that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  won't go beyond giving clear hints at a prospective end of QE while waiting until the July meeting before providing full clarity. On Wednesday, Praet sent a strong signal that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  could decide as soon as next week to wind down its EUR30 billion a month bond-buying program. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT - How long the uptrend in Singapore's residential real estate can last amid the potential of fresh market-cooling measures and a rising interest-rate market are concerns which look overdone to \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  . Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly flagged its concerns on the recent swift price gains and high economic-system liquidity. Before the past year's increases, prices were muted the prior 4 years following prior market-cooling steps. But \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says the upcycle is still "early" with rentals yet to turn around. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  's foreign reserves dipped CHF16.3 billion ($16.6 billion) to CHF740.9 billion last month, almost certainly tied to Italy and its political uncertainty which triggered a sharp drop in the euro-franc exchange rate. That, in turn, lowered the value of the bank's pile of euro-denominated assets in francs. The euro has rebounded a bit so far this month, but May's action was a big hit to the SNB's balance sheet. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0720 GMT - Some analysts say the dollar should rise again medium-term, but short-term, euro strength will likely dominate, say ING analysts. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to discuss quantitative easing at its June meeting and could hint when investors should expect its end, strengthening the euro. EUR/USD on Thursday is up 0.4% at 1.1816 and the DXY dollar index falls by 0.2% to 93.44. If DXY falls below 93.30 on Thursday, steps toward 92.80 are likely, ING says. However, "it feels too early to sound the all-clear on the investment environment or to be talking about a renewed benign dollar decline," ING adds.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT - German manufacturing orders fell for the fourth straight month in April, with total orders down a staggering 2.5% from March, dampening hopes of a pickup in economic growth in the second quarter. "The development does not look good," says \uc2 Thomas Gitzel\uc2 , an economist at Lichtenstein-based VP Bank. Rising trade tension between the world's leading economies may have caused some companies to postpone orders. "It could well be that the slack in the German economy is stronger than previously thought," Mr. Gitzel adds. (nina.adam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT - \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  expects French government bonds, known as OATs, to make up for recent cheapening and firm after Thursday's heavy supply is absorbed. "When the heavy supply has been digested, we would expect renewed tightening after the recent cheapening of France," says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 's chief analyst \uc2 Jakob Christensen\uc2 . The French Treasury Agency will auction EUR8 billion to EUR9 billion in four long-dated bonds. Its offer includes the launch of the new 0.75% November 2028 OAT as well as the reopening of the 5.50% April 2029 OAT, the 1.50% May 2031 OAT and the 1.25% May 2036 OAT. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 07, 2018 04:17 ET (08:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180607ee67000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
