\par\par \b \uc2 Any Euro-Negative Impact from Italian Coalition Should Be Limited, SocGen Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2018\par 12:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1042 GMT - The tentative coalition agreement between Italy's hard-right Northern League and anti-establishment 5-Star Movement is helping euro rise as this removes risks of new elections. Any negative impact on the euro should be minimal as long as the spread between Italian bonds and German bunds remain at current levels, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "had the BTP [Italian bonds] market's back since 2012, and unless we start to doubt that this can continue, any \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact will be limited." EUR/USD up 0.3% at 1.1981. The Italian-German 10-year bond spread rises slightly to 132.50, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1040 GMT - If Germany's quarterly growth rate was to slow to 0.3% in 1Q, as a Wall Street Journal poll indicates, then it would mark the weakest rate since 3Q 2016. If growth were to slow to 0.2%, it would be the lowest rate since 1Q 2015, while a flat or slightly negative outcome, as expected by \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 , would be the weakest result since 2Q 2014, when German GDP fell by 0.2% compared with the previous quarter. Germany's statistics body is due to publish a flash GDP estimate at 0600 GMT on Tuesday. (nina.adam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1010 GMT - China's banking and insurance regulator says it will keep up "high pressure" on violation of laws and regulations through imposition of hefty penalties, according to a statement about a meeting held Monday. The regulator says it still faces severe challenges in minimizing financial risks in the banking and insurance sectors and will rely on a preventive approach. ( grace.zhu@wsj.com )\par \par \uc2 1009 GMT - German first-quarter GDP figures, due Tuesday, are expected to show a sharp slowdown in economic activity, with the annualized growth rate seen down at 1.2% compared with 2.5% in the fourth quarter of 2017, according to a Wall Street Journal poll of 16 economists. A period of cold weather, the flu wave and strikes in the engineering and metals industries contributed to the slowdown, economists say. While some positive payback appears likely in the second quarter, economists say they are increasingly questioning their buoyant full-year GDP forecasts following the wobbly start to 2018. (nina.adam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - Fresh declines for the rupee to start the week, helped on by exit polls from elections in the southern Indian state of Karnataka pointing to a hung assembly. Results are due tomorrow, and broker \uc2 Kotak\uc2  says a narrow win for the main opposition won't upset markets much. That as 2 traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  say there's chatter of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the rupee, which is at fresh 16-month lows in getting toward INR67.50/dollar. India's Sensex was barely higher ahead of the close. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 GMT - Sterling rises on Monday on the back of dollar weakness, with GBP/USD up 0.3% at 1.3583, and this week it may go up even further, to between 1.37 and 1.38, if Tuesday's U.K. labor market data are "constructive," ING says. According to a WSJ poll of economists, average earnings for the three months to March are expected to have risen 2.9% in the U.K., compared with a 2.8% rise in the corresponding period. EUR/GBP is up 0.1% at 0.8823. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT - Indian Finance Minister Arun Jaitley, who was being treated for kidney-related problems the past couple months, underwent a successful renal transplant today, said the \uc2 All India Institute of Medical Sciences\uc2  . Jaitley is responsible for formulating policies to prop up the economy. Due to bad health, he canceled participation at key events abroad and was working from home. (rajesh.roy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT - The euro is rising on Monday on the agreement between Italy's 5-Star Movement and far-right League to form a government. The news isn't likely to boost the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much further, though it does help create a bottom for EUR/USD, says ING. "With the latest positioning data showing net EUR longs stabilising...there are tentative signs of a bottoming out in EUR/USD." EUR/USD has been falling for three weeks up to recently due to broad-based dollar strength. On Monday, EUR/USD is up by 0.4% at 1.1986, also because the dollar is retracing some of its previous gains. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT - Though the ringgit rebounded strongly after initial weakness as Malaysian markets reopened this morning after the election, cracks are "likely to appear in the ruling 4-party coalition, which along with fiscal concerns is likely to keep the ringgit at risk," says Charu Chanana at Continuum Economics. She adds \uc2 Anwar Ibrahim\uc2  poised to become PM is likely to keep markets jittery short-term. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT - Bitcoin continues to fall on Monday after 13% of losses last week, mainly due to criticizing comments from billionaires such as Warren Buffet and Bill Gates , and the fact that South Korean's biggest cryptocurrency exchange UPbit had invited guests--prosecutors who raided its offices. According to Factset, bitcoin latest trades down 4% at $8,357. Nasdaq stock exchange Chief Executive \uc2 Adena Friedman\uc2  saying bitcoin could be used as a globalized payment mechanism. Meanwhile, the Winklevoss brothers, founders of the \uc2 Gemini\uc2  crypto exchange, won a patent for a trading system for ETPs tied to cryptocurrencies. Ethereum is also falling, by 5% to $697, and \uc2 Litecoin\uc2  is down 6% at $136. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT - The euro is rising on Monday, mainly due to dollar weakness, but also because Italy's 5-Star Movement and far-right League have agreed to form a government. Although there may be concerns about a government made up of anti-establishment parties, this "reduces any lingering risks of another Italian election and ongoing political impasse," says ING. However, "any follow-through to EUR price action should be minimal as we still await further details on policy proposals, the name of Italy's next Prime Minister and key ministerial positions." EUR/USD up 0.3% at 1.1973. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT - ING recommends tactically selling the dollar, given that the monetary policy divergence theme is unlikely to be of much benefit for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given "softer" U.S. inflation, while "fears of economic under-performance in the rest of the world look overblown now." ING adds: "As such, we do believe that the theme of MEGA: Make Everyone (else) Great Again - that worked so well at the start of the year in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - will come back in play." EUR/USD is up 0.3% at 1.1977. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 14, 2018 06:42 ET (10:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180514ee5e0017d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
