\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL ECONOMY-Asia sees signs of export weakness before key U.S.-China trade talks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,003 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2018\par 07:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Business surveys show domestic demand holding up, but exports weakening in Asia\par \par \uc2 * High-level U.S.-China talks this week watched closely\par \par \uc2 * U.S. yields, oil prices bite; trade war remains major risk\par \par \uc2 By Marius Zaharia\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 2 (Reuters) - Manufacturing activity remained relatively solid in major Asian economies such as China and Japan in April, but exports showed signs of weakness across the region, a worrying development given heightened Sino-U.S. trade tensions.\par \par \uc2 High-level U.S. and Chinese officials meet in China this week, with trade expected to be top of the agenda as both sides have threatened reciprocal tariffs on hundreds of billions of dollars worth of imports.\par \par \uc2 The meetings' outcome could be crucial for the outlook for Asian exporters as Purchasing Managers Index (PMI) surveys of factory activity are already pointing to a slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing is a cyclical soft patch in exports after a very strong rise last year," said Dong Chen, senior Asia economist at Pictet Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 "Without considering the potential trade war, we're not very worried about it, we think it's fairly normal. But if we think about the trade war ... then the outlook is quite uncertain. It could potentially bring more damage going forward."\par \par \uc2 Tensions between the United States and China escalated earlier this year, when President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatened tariffs on up to $150 billion of Chinese goods to punish China over unfair joint-venture and intellectual property practices.\par \par \uc2 China, which denies allegations it coerces technology transfers through these channels, has threatened equal retaliation, including tariffs on U.S. soybeans and aircraft.\par \par \uc2 A Trump administration delegation including Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2 , Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , Commerce Secretary \uc2 Wilbur Ross\uc2  and advisers \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  and \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  is heading to China for talks.\par \par \uc2 Ross said on Tuesday Trump was prepared to levy tariffs if the delegation did not reach a settlement to reduce trade imbalances. Lighthizer said he was not looking to negotiate changes to China's state-driven economic system, but would seek to expose it to more foreign competition.\par \par \uc2 "The agreement to talk, even if not yet to begin formal negotiations, is ... a step in the right direction, albeit one that brings a risk of negative market reaction when followed by inevitable disappointment as talks enter a more protracted phase," said Jon Harrison, managing director for emerging markets macro strategy at TS Lombard.\par \par \uc2 ALREADY LOSING MOMENTUM\par \par \uc2 The momentum in global trade, which has been responsible for much of the world's growth in the past year or two, may be already weakening, as firms that are part of supply chains that could be hit by tariffs may be ordering fewer components for products they sell overseas, some analysts say.\par \par \uc2 China's Caixin/Markit PMI climbed to 51.1 in April from a four-month low of 51.0 in March, and topped economists' forecast for a modest slowdown to 50.9.\par \par \uc2 The survey's findings suggested continued strength in the domestic economy, where consumption was a major growth driver in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 But a sub-index on export orders shrank for the first time since November 2016. An official PMI survey on Monday also showed external orders slowed last month.\par \par \uc2 Japan saw a similar trend. The Markit/Nikkei PMI rose to 53.8 in April versus a flash reading of 53.3 and a final 53.1 in the previous month. But growth in export orders slowed sharply to only marginal levels due to a stronger yen.\par \par \uc2 Factory activity in South Korea contracted for a second month in April, with both domestic and export orders shrinking. Some firms said they had scaled back production due to weaker global demand, particularly for electronics and autos.\par \par \uc2 Data on Wednesday showed South Korean exports declined in April for the first time in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 In Taiwan, another Asian hi-tech powerhouse, factory growth slowed to six-month lows.\par \par \uc2 Business growth in the United States and Europe, while still strong, has slowed to more modest levels with U.S. manufacturers complaining about rising commodity prices in anticipation of trade tariffs.\par \par \uc2 On the positive side, domestic orders remained strong and a long spell of sharply higher input prices appears to be levelling off.\par \par \uc2 This suggests many Asian central banks can leave interest rates low to keep supporting their economies, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The big picture is that inflation in most countries in the region is set to stay benign, giving their central banks scope to keep monetary policy accommodative to support growth," said \uc2 Krystal Tan\uc2 , Asia economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 DOUBLE-WHAMMY\par \par \uc2 A resurgent dollar against emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also restore some of Asian exporters' competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 However, that may not apply to the trio of Asian countries with trade deficits -- India, Indonesia and Philippines -- which have recently taken a one-two punch.\par \par \uc2 A rise in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields to 3 percent and expectations of further \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate hikes have increased concerns about potential portfolio outflows, while oil prices at 3-1/2 year highs around $75 per barrel have significantly raised import costs.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to hold rates steady this week, but will likely further encourage expectations of a June rate hike, which could potentially keep pushing U.S. yields higher.\par \par \uc2 All three countries saw their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets selling off in the past week and policymakers are reacting.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it was prepared to raise rates to defend the rupiah and that it has recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew a restriction that allowed foreign investors to only invest in corporate and government debt with tenures of three years or more.\par \par \uc2 "India and Southeast Asia are particularly at risk if oil prices rise further," TS Lombard's Harrison said. (Reporting by Marius Zaharia; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-2T06:48:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180502ee52007sl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
