\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,083 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2018\par 05:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT - Julian Evans-Pritchard at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  has been expecting a slowdown in China, and he still does despite better-than-expected readings today on GDP and retail sales. He anticipates tighter fiscal policy and slower credit creation to continue weighing on broader activity. That as there's skepticism about China's data. "We don't think China's economy is expanding as rapidly as the official figures claim." (lin.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT - While India was the new member of Treasury's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -manipulation watch list, Smartkarma's Vinay Patel says natural-gas imports from the US could change that. India met 2 of the 3 criteria used by the Treasury to be deemed a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator--a trade surplus of more than $20 billion with America and noted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  received its first shipment of US shale oil in late 2017, and the first LNG shipment was loaded just last month. Some $2 billion each of LNG and oil imports should help get India's trade surplus with the US below $20 billion. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT - \uc2 BNZ\uc2  says net longs regarding the New Zealand dollar have grown to a point that a pullback is inevitable. While it's not sure when that will come, now might be a good opportunity to take a contrarian view, the bank adds. While lightening up the kiwi versus the greenback has merit, \uc2 BNZ\uc2  thinks a less-risky proposition for now is to go long AUD/NZD and short NZD/CAD. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT - China's economy has become more resilient than before in dealing with rising trade tensions with the US, says Xing Zhihong, a spokesman for the National Bureau of Statistics. "Trade frictions won't beat the Chinese economy and won't change the course of China's economic growth," which is heading in a right direction, he says at a briefing. Xing added that China's economic structure--more reliant on domestic demand--can help stabilize the economy in face of external shocks. He notes consumption has been the biggest growth engine for China's economy the past 5 years. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT - China's economy is holding steady for now, but Beijing's debt-reduction policies have lead to higher interest rates which could curb 2H growth, says \uc2 OCBC\uc2  economist \uc2 Tommy Xie\uc2 . "Things are fine now, but looking ahead we should see more headwinds." Rising trade tensions with the US haven't shown a big impact yet. Meanwhile, "optimistic growth momentum at the beginning of the year should be able to offer some kind of buffer for Beijing." (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT - The narrative of Asian export resurgence may need some scrutiny. \uc2 Nomura\uc2 's in-house index predicting regional exports excluding Japan 3 months out points to weaker growth. That as March data from China, India, Indonesia, South Korea and Taiwan all showed 3-month moving averages slow regarding export growth to 11% on-year from 15% through February. But \uc2 Nomura\uc2  acknowledges, as do some peers, that the Lunar New Year holiday in February may have skewed numbers as production in China dropped then. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT - The \uc2 HKMA\uc2  will defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg at all costs, says Daiwa, and has plenty of ammo to do so. But the investment bank adds that the cost could be quite high depending on the size of outflows. It notes monetary data shows that total net inflows peaked at US$355 billion in May and had dropped 4.5% through February. With the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Thursday to prop up the HK dollar, Daiwa estimates inflows have now fallen 8% since the end of May. It also sees 3-month Hibor ending 2018 at 3.1%, versus 2.1% previously, after the interbank lending rate started 2018 at 1.25%. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT - As expected, the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's tune remains the same. Minutes of the Aussie \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting 2 weeks ago signals the next move in interest rates is likely to be higher. But don't hold your breath on that happening soon as wage-growth remains dank. A case for hiking is a year-end thing at the earliest. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT - China guided the yuan stronger against the dollar on today's fix, putting the trading midpoint at CNY6.2771, a 2-week high and just below a post-devaluation best. The fix was CNY6.2884 yesterday. The yuan gained onshore Monday, sitting at CNY6.2811 as of the 4:30pm close. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT - Superlong JGB yields ticked higher after fresh gains on Treasurys. Still, the market is "beginning to pull out of the risk-off flow" that began earlier this year, says Naka Matsuzawa, rates specialist with \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  . Treasury yields for now peaked last month. For JGBs, 20- and 30-year yields are up a half-basis point at 0.51% and 0.705%, respectively. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @SuryatapaB)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT - While the yen has eased of late, there's still plenty of trader support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Oanda's Stephen Innes. All it might take for the yen to rebound is more political concern in Japan. Abe could be at risk, and he's suffered another setback with the Finance Ministry opening an investigation into sexual-harassment allegations against a top official. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT - Singapore's March exports showed a surprise decline, but is unlikely to trigger alarm. Only 2 out of 9 economists in the WSJ's poll had predicted a decline, and none as much as the 2.7% on year fall in exports of locally-produced goods reported this morning. The government agency that releases the data attributed the decline to a high base of comparison from a year ago, and notes that the pace of decline of both electronics and non-electronics slowed in March. The data appears in line with the consensus view that Singapore's economy will level off this year after last year's bounce.(gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 16, 2018 23:30 ET (03:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180417ee4h000a9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
