\par\par \b \uc2 India's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Won't Lower Rates to Slow Rupee's Gains -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2017\par 09:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0731 GMT - Investors are wondering if the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  will try to lower real interest rates to slow the rupee's gains on the back of strong capital inflows. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says it won't and would rather buy dollars to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under its flexible inflation-targeting regime, the RBI may take action to account for the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation on inflation, but not as a move to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  per se, MS says. "As capital flows remain strong, we expect the RBI to respond with further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it says. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are already at an all-time high of more than $400 billion. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT - Demand may be tepid for the new 1.25%, August 2048 bund that the German Finance Agency is set to auction with an initial volume of EUR2 billion on Wednesday, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  and KBC analysts say. This expectation has been given because grey markets--where bonds trade ahead of their launch--indicate that the bund is in line with its estimated fair value, rather than with a pre-auction cheapening. "We expect weak demand...unless Germany is willing to pay a heavy new issue premium," say KBC analysts. In addition, the offer volume is higher than the usual EUR1 billion at tap auctions. However, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  rates strategist Michael Leister adds that German life insurers' appetite for the ultra-long bund should increase over the coming weeks. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT - Bond investors have been bracing themselves for today's Fed statement and updated economic projections, with 10-year Treasury yields up for 7 straight sessions, the longest-such run since March. That move came ahead of a Fed meeting which for a time investors weren't sure that a rate hike would come. One definitely isn't today; instead the rollout of details on balance-sheet shrinkage is widely anticipated. And the yield gain for the Treasury benchmark hasn't been huge at 18 basis points. The move has also partially undone the Treasury rally which pushed the 10-year yield to a 2017 low of 2.06% on a 3pm ET basis. Potentially most important today is whether most policy makers still expect a 3rd rate hike this year and 3 more in 2018. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0720 GMT - New bond supply and the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's much-anticipated meeting are two factors that should move 10-year \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds Wednesday. The most interesting element of the Fed announcement will be the guidance on the future path of interest rates, as an announcement on the reduction of its balance sheet is a "mere formality by now", \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  rate strategist Michael Leister says. Forecasts about how much and how quickly rates could rise may be revised lower, capping the balance sheet reduction-spurred upside in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, says \uc2 Landesbank Hessen-Thueringen\uc2  strategist Viola Julien in a note. But before the Fed takes over, Germany will issue EUR2 billion of 30-year debt Wednesday. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT - The U.S. dollar is marginally down before the Federal Open Market Committee rate announcement at 1800 GMT. EUR/USD is slightly up at 1.2010. The Fed is expected to announce a reduction of its balance sheet, but this has largely been signaled, and so it should have less of an effect on the dollar. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  even says that balance sheet normalization shouldn't be considered a type of monetary policy tightening, and is therefore "hardly relevant for the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." ING says that risks are to the downside for the dollar because there is "a likely shift lower in the distribution of dots given the growing division among FOMC members and the rising dovish camp." (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT - The dollar could well end the year near current levels at around CNY6.5 before falling a bit further versus the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2018 given an expected weak dollar outlook, says Oxford Economics' Louis Kuijs. While sentiment on the yuan and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves has improved markedly, net financial capital outflows remain sizeable. As such, a "material relaxation" of Beijing's policy stance on outflows is unlikely any time soon. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT - London shares are tipped to open slightly down as geopolitical tension re-emerges and as traders sit tight ahead of a day of heavyweight economic news. Dealers expect the FTSE 100 Index to begin the session two points adrift at 7273 after U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatened to destroy North Korea if its leader \uc2 Kim Jong-un\uc2 , whom he dubbed 'Rocket Man,' attacked the U.S. or its allies. Later Wednesday, the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to keep U.S. interest rates unchanged, though it could announce the start of balance sheet reduction. \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  says it could begin that process in November or December, with a rate rise possible in December. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT - Nordic markets are seen opening little-changed Wednesday with IG calling the OMXS30 flat at around 1584. "Although U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's speech to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  yesterday was hard on the rhetoric - in particular against North Korea - financial markets remained relatively calm," says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . U.S. equities ended the day slightly higher and in Asia this morning, Chinese and Japanese equity indices are also trading higher while most other regional indices trade lower, it adds. "With little in the data calendar today, we are probably in for another quiet session as investors await the FOMC meeting and \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's speech tonight." OMXS30 closed at 1584.43, OMXN40 at 1601.92 and OBX at 694.04. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT - \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  expects the Netherlands' government bond issuance in 2018 to be lower than in 2017, driven by robust economic growth and possible additional divestment of state-owned companies, says Kim Liu, senior fixed income strategist at the bank. "The starting position of the borrowing requirement is quite favorable," he says. \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  forecasts the country's 2018 borrowing requirement at EUR54 billion, of which EUR26 billion would be financed via government bond issuance. This is lower than the \uc2 Dutch State Treasury Agency\uc2 's government bond issuance target of EUR30-EUR35 billion for 2017. Mr. Liu expects the DSTA's 2017 bond issuance to come in at around EUR31 billion, thus closer to the lower end of the range. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT - The fourth quarter of the year is traditionally supportive for Danish government bonds, attributable to redemptions and coupon payment in government and mortgage bonds and the very modest supply, says \uc2 Danske\uc2 . This year is no exception with payments from mortgage bonds on Oct. 1 and from government bonds on Nov. 15. Arne Lohmann Rasmussen, \uc2 Danske\uc2 's head of fixed income research, says that abundant liquidity has often resulted in the outperformance of Danish government bonds in the fourth quarter. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT - The New Zealand dollar jumped sharply following the release of a One News Colmar Brunton Poll that showed support for the ruling National Party has increased significantly over the past week, with the party now holding 46% of support just 4 days out from Saturday's election, up 6 percentage points. New Zealand's second-biggest party, Labour, has just 37% support, down 7 percentage points on the previous poll. Still, New Zealand has a complex political system that lends itself to coalition governments making it unlikely one party will win a clear majority. The NZD/USD is last trading up 0.7% at 0.7365. The pair was trading at 0.7310 before the spike. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT - \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  expects the \uc2 Dutch State Treasury Agency\uc2  to announce Friday that it will lower its borrowing requirement for 2017, thanks to favorable public finances and privatizations, says Kim Liu, senior fixed-income strategist. The agency, or DSTA, is to publish its quarterly funding plan and outlook Friday. The DSTA currently estimates a EUR56.8 billion borrowing need and all else being equal, the better-than-expected cash balance would lower that by EUR6 billion to EUR50.8 billion, Mr. Liu says. \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  previously forecast that the DSTA's 2017 government-bond issuance would be around EUR31 billion, close to the lower end of the EUR30 billion-EUR35 billion full-year target. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 20, 2017 03:31 ET (07:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170920ed9k000i5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
